L['dl-w2-l1'] = {
  duration_mins: 50,
  sections: [
    { type: 'text', body: '<h2>Weight Initialisation & Batch Normalisation</h2><p>Two of the most impactful practical advances in training deep networks. Without them, training stacks of 20+ layers was nearly impossible � gradients would vanish or explode before reaching early layers. With them, we can train networks hundreds of layers deep.</p>' },
    { type: 'text', body: '<h3>Why Initialisation Matters</h3><p>All weights at zero: every neuron computes the same gradient � the network never breaks symmetry and learns nothing. All weights large: activations saturate (sigmoid/tanh ? �1), gradients vanish. <strong>Good initialisation</strong> keeps signal and gradient variance stable across layers.</p>' },
    { type: 'code', lang: 'python', src: 'import torch\nimport torch.nn as nn\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\ndef forward_signal_variance(init_fn, n_layers=20, n_neurons=256):\n    """Track activation variance through a deep network."""\n    torch.manual_seed(42)\n    x = torch.randn(1000, n_neurons)\n    vars_per_layer = [x.var().item()]\n    for _ in range(n_layers):\n        W = init_fn(n_neurons, n_neurons)\n        x = torch.relu(x @ W)\n        vars_per_layer.append(x.var().item())\n    return vars_per_layer\n\n# Xavier (Glorot) init � designed for tanh, divides by sqrt(fan_in + fan_out)\ndef xavier(fan_in, fan_out):\n    std = (2 / (fan_in + fan_out)) ** 0.5\n    return torch.randn(fan_in, fan_out) * std\n\n# He (Kaiming) init � designed for ReLU, divides by sqrt(fan_in / 2)\ndef he(fan_in, fan_out):\n    std = (2 / fan_in) ** 0.5\n    return torch.randn(fan_in, fan_out) * std\n\n# Naive (too large) init\ndef naive(fan_in, fan_out):\n    return torch.randn(fan_in, fan_out)\n\nplt.figure(figsize=(10, 5))\nfor name, fn in [("Naive", naive), ("Xavier", xavier), ("He (Kaiming)", he)]:\n    variances = forward_signal_variance(fn)\n    plt.plot(variances, label=name)\nplt.xlabel("Layer"); plt.ylabel("Activation variance")\nplt.title("Signal variance through 20 layers by initialisation strategy")\nplt.legend(); plt.yscale("log"); plt.grid(alpha=.3); plt.tight_layout(); plt.show()' },
    { type: 'text', body: '<h3>Batch Normalisation</h3><p>Proposed by Ioffe & Szegedy (2015), BatchNorm solves the <em>internal covariate shift</em> problem: as weights update, the distribution of each layer\'s input shifts, forcing downstream layers to continually adapt. BatchNorm normalises each mini-batch\'s activations to zero mean and unit variance, then applies learnable scale ? and shift �:</p><p style="text-align:center;padding:.5rem;background:var(--fog);border-radius:6px;">x^ = (x - �_B) / v(s�_B + e),  y = ? x^ + �</p><p>Effects:</p><ul><li>Allows much higher learning rates (training is faster)</li><li>Acts as regulariser � batch statistics add noise (reduces need for Dropout)</li><li>Reduces sensitivity to initialisation</li><li>Enables training of very deep networks</li></ul>' },
    { type: 'code', lang: 'python', src: 'import torch\nimport torch.nn as nn\n\n# Manual BatchNorm to build intuition\nclass ManualBatchNorm(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, num_features, eps=1e-5, momentum=0.1):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.gamma = nn.Parameter(torch.ones(num_features))\n        self.beta  = nn.Parameter(torch.zeros(num_features))\n        self.register_buffer("running_mean", torch.zeros(num_features))\n        self.register_buffer("running_var",  torch.ones(num_features))\n        self.eps = eps; self.momentum = momentum\n\n    def forward(self, x):\n        if self.training:\n            mu  = x.mean(0); var = x.var(0, unbiased=False)\n            self.running_mean = (1-self.momentum)*self.running_mean + self.momentum*mu.detach()\n            self.running_var  = (1-self.momentum)*self.running_var  + self.momentum*var.detach()\n        else:\n            mu = self.running_mean; var = self.running_var\n        x_hat = (x - mu) / (var + self.eps).sqrt()\n        return self.gamma * x_hat + self.beta\n\n# Practical: BN goes BEFORE activation (or after � both used)\nmodel = nn.Sequential(\n    nn.Linear(784, 512),\n    nn.BatchNorm1d(512),   # normalise, then activate\n    nn.ReLU(),\n    nn.Linear(512, 10)\n)\nprint(model)' },
    { type: 'warn', title: 'BatchNorm + small batch size', body: 'BatchNorm breaks with batch size 1 (variance undefined) and degrades with batch sizes < 8. For small batches: use LayerNorm (NLP), GroupNorm (object detection), or InstanceNorm (style transfer). For transformers, LayerNorm is always preferred.' }
  ]
};

L['dl-w2-l2'] = {
  duration_mins: 50,
  sections: [
    { type: 'text', body: '<h2>Regularisation � Dropout, Weight Decay & Data Augmentation</h2><p>Deep networks are overparameterised � a 10-million-parameter model trained on 50,000 examples has far more parameters than data. Without regularisation, it memorises training examples instead of learning patterns. Three complementary techniques address this.</p>' },
    { type: 'text', body: '<h3>Dropout</h3><p>Proposed by Srivastava et al. (2014): during training, randomly zero out each neuron\'s activation with probability p (typically 0.1�0.5). This prevents co-adaptation � neurons cannot rely on specific other neurons, forcing more redundant, robust representations. At test time, dropout is off and all activations are used (scaled by 1-p to keep expected values equal).</p><p>Intuitively: training with dropout is like training an exponential ensemble of 2? subnetworks sharing weights.</p>' },
    { type: 'code', lang: 'python', src: 'import torch\nimport torch.nn as nn\n\n# Dropout applied correctly\nclass RegularisedMLP(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, input_dim=784, dropout=0.3):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.net = nn.Sequential(\n            nn.Linear(input_dim, 512),\n            nn.BatchNorm1d(512),\n            nn.ReLU(),\n            nn.Dropout(dropout),      # applied after activation\n            nn.Linear(512, 256),\n            nn.BatchNorm1d(256),\n            nn.ReLU(),\n            nn.Dropout(dropout / 2),  # less dropout closer to output\n            nn.Linear(256, 10)\n        )\n    def forward(self, x):\n        return self.net(x)\n\n# Verify dropout is active in train mode, disabled in eval mode\nmodel = RegularisedMLP()\nx = torch.randn(4, 784)\n\nmodel.train()\nout1 = model(x); out2 = model(x)\nprint("Train mode � outputs differ (dropout is stochastic):", not torch.allclose(out1, out2))\n\nmodel.eval()\nout3 = model(x); out4 = model(x)\nprint("Eval mode � outputs identical (no dropout):", torch.allclose(out3, out4))' },
    { type: 'text', body: '<h3>Weight Decay (L2 Regularisation)</h3><p>Adds a penalty term ?||W||� to the loss, encouraging small weights. In PyTorch optimisers, set <code>weight_decay=1e-4</code> (typical range 1e-5 to 1e-2). Use AdamW not Adam � AdamW applies weight decay directly to weights (correct), while Adam applies it through the gradient (subtly incorrect due to adaptive scaling).</p>' },
    { type: 'text', body: '<h3>Data Augmentation</h3><p>The most powerful regulariser: artificially expand the training dataset by applying label-preserving transformations. For images: random crops, horizontal flips, colour jitter, rotation. For text: synonym replacement, back-translation. The network sees different views of each example every epoch � it cannot memorise.</p>' },
    { type: 'code', lang: 'python', src: 'from torchvision import transforms\n\n# Standard augmentation pipeline for image classification\ntrain_transform = transforms.Compose([\n    transforms.RandomCrop(32, padding=4),        # crop with 4-pixel padding\n    transforms.RandomHorizontalFlip(p=0.5),      # flip 50% of images\n    transforms.ColorJitter(brightness=0.2,       # slightly randomise colour\n                           contrast=0.2,\n                           saturation=0.2),\n    transforms.ToTensor(),\n    transforms.Normalize((0.4914, 0.4822, 0.4465),  # CIFAR-10 mean\n                         (0.2470, 0.2435, 0.2616))  # CIFAR-10 std\n])\n\n# Test transform � NO augmentation, only normalisation\ntest_transform = transforms.Compose([\n    transforms.ToTensor(),\n    transforms.Normalize((0.4914, 0.4822, 0.4465), (0.2470, 0.2435, 0.2616))\n])\n\n# Advanced: RandAugment (automatically searches augmentation policies)\nfrom torchvision.transforms import RandAugment\nadvanced_transform = transforms.Compose([\n    RandAugment(num_ops=2, magnitude=9),  # 2 random ops from 14 possible\n    transforms.ToTensor(),\n    transforms.Normalize((0.4914, 0.4822, 0.4465), (0.2470, 0.2435, 0.2616))\n])' },
    { type: 'tip', title: 'Regularisation rule of thumb', body: 'Add regularisation in this order until overfitting is controlled: (1) weight decay (free, always on), (2) data augmentation (always helps if augmentations are valid), (3) dropout (tune p, start at 0.2). Only reduce model capacity as a last resort � a larger regularised model almost always beats a smaller unregularised one.' }
  ]
};

L['dl-w2-l3'] = {
  duration_mins: 50,
  sections: [
    { type: 'text', body: '<h2>The Training Loop � Production Patterns</h2><p>A robust training loop is the backbone of any deep learning project. This lesson covers the full production pattern: checkpointing, early stopping, mixed precision training, gradient clipping, and experiment logging.</p>' },
    { type: 'code', lang: 'python', src: 'import torch\nimport torch.nn as nn\nfrom torch.cuda.amp import GradScaler, autocast\nimport time\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nclass Trainer:\n    def __init__(self, model, train_loader, val_loader, optimiser, scheduler,\n                 criterion, device="cpu", checkpoint_dir="checkpoints/",\n                 patience=5, use_amp=True):\n        self.model        = model.to(device)\n        self.train_loader = train_loader\n        self.val_loader   = val_loader\n        self.optimiser    = optimiser\n        self.scheduler    = scheduler\n        self.criterion    = criterion\n        self.device       = device\n        self.ckpt_dir     = Path(checkpoint_dir)\n        self.ckpt_dir.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\n        self.patience     = patience\n        self.scaler       = GradScaler(enabled=use_amp and device=="cuda")\n        self.use_amp      = use_amp and device=="cuda"\n        self.history      = {"train_loss": [], "val_loss": [], "val_acc": []}\n        self.best_val_loss = float("inf")\n        self.no_improve    = 0\n\n    def train_epoch(self):\n        self.model.train()\n        total_loss, total = 0, 0\n        for x, y in self.train_loader:\n            x, y = x.to(self.device), y.to(self.device)\n            self.optimiser.zero_grad()\n            with autocast(enabled=self.use_amp):\n                out  = self.model(x)\n                loss = self.criterion(out, y)\n            self.scaler.scale(loss).backward()\n            self.scaler.unscale_(self.optimiser)\n            torch.nn.utils.clip_grad_norm_(self.model.parameters(), max_norm=1.0)\n            self.scaler.step(self.optimiser)\n            self.scaler.update()\n            if self.scheduler: self.scheduler.step()\n            total_loss += loss.item() * len(y); total += len(y)\n        return total_loss / total\n\n    @torch.no_grad()\n    def evaluate(self):\n        self.model.eval()\n        total_loss, correct, total = 0, 0, 0\n        for x, y in self.val_loader:\n            x, y = x.to(self.device), y.to(self.device)\n            out  = self.model(x)\n            loss = self.criterion(out, y)\n            total_loss += loss.item() * len(y)\n            correct    += (out.argmax(1) == y).sum().item()\n            total      += len(y)\n        return total_loss / total, correct / total\n\n    def fit(self, epochs):\n        for epoch in range(epochs):\n            t0 = time.time()\n            tr_loss          = self.train_epoch()\n            va_loss, va_acc  = self.evaluate()\n            elapsed          = time.time() - t0\n            self.history["train_loss"].append(tr_loss)\n            self.history["val_loss"].append(va_loss)\n            self.history["val_acc"].append(va_acc)\n            print(f"Ep {epoch+1:3d}/{epochs} | "\n                  f"train={tr_loss:.4f} val={va_loss:.4f} acc={va_acc:.4f} "\n                  f"lr={self.optimiser.param_groups[0][\'lr\']:.2e} [{elapsed:.1f}s]")\n            if va_loss < self.best_val_loss:\n                self.best_val_loss = va_loss\n                self.no_improve = 0\n                torch.save({"epoch": epoch, "model": self.model.state_dict(),\n                            "optimiser": self.optimiser.state_dict(),\n                            "val_loss": va_loss},\n                           self.ckpt_dir / "best.pt")\n                print(f"  Checkpoint saved (val_loss={va_loss:.6f})")\n            else:\n                self.no_improve += 1\n                if self.no_improve >= self.patience:\n                    print(f"Early stopping at epoch {epoch+1}")\n                    break\n        return self.history' },
    { type: 'text', body: '<h3>Mixed Precision Training (AMP)</h3><p>By default, PyTorch uses 32-bit floats (FP32). Mixed precision uses 16-bit floats (FP16) for forward/backward passes and 32-bit for weight updates. The result: ~2x speedup and ~2x memory savings on modern GPUs, with no accuracy loss. The <code>GradScaler</code> handles numerical stability by scaling the loss to prevent underflow in FP16 gradients.</p><p>Enable with just three additions to your training loop: <code>autocast()</code> context manager, <code>scaler.scale(loss).backward()</code>, and <code>scaler.step() / scaler.update()</code>.</p>' },
    { type: 'text', body: '<h3>Checkpoint Loading & Resumption</h3>' },
    { type: 'code', lang: 'python', src: '# Load best checkpoint and resume training or evaluate\nimport torch\n\ndef load_checkpoint(model, optimiser, checkpoint_path, device="cpu"):\n    ckpt = torch.load(checkpoint_path, map_location=device)\n    model.load_state_dict(ckpt["model"])\n    if optimiser and "optimiser" in ckpt:\n        optimiser.load_state_dict(ckpt["optimiser"])\n    start_epoch = ckpt.get("epoch", 0) + 1\n    val_loss    = ckpt.get("val_loss", float("inf"))\n    print(f"Loaded checkpoint from epoch {start_epoch-1}, val_loss={val_loss:.4f}")\n    return start_epoch, val_loss' },
    { type: 'tip', title: 'Gradient clipping is non-negotiable', body: 'clip_grad_norm_(parameters, max_norm=1.0) prevents exploding gradients � especially important for RNNs, transformers, and any model trained with high learning rates. A gradient spike can corrupt weights permanently (even one bad update can push weights into a region from which the network cannot recover).' }
  ]
};

L['dl-w2-l4'] = {
  duration_mins: 45,
  sections: [
    { type: 'text', body: '<h2>Debugging Neural Networks</h2><p>Training a deep network and seeing "loss not decreasing" or "NaN after epoch 3" provides almost no information. Systematic debugging � checking data, gradients, weights, and predictions at each stage � is the skill that separates practitioners who ship models from those who abandon them.</p>' },
    { type: 'text', body: '<h3>The Debugging Hierarchy</h3><ol><li><strong>Overfit one batch first</strong>: the most powerful sanity check. Train on 1-8 samples for many epochs � loss should go to near zero. If it does not, your model has a bug, not a generalisation problem.</li><li><strong>Check gradient flow</strong>: if gradients are zero or NaN, the network cannot learn.</li><li><strong>Check weight distributions</strong>: dead layers (all zeros), saturated activations (all �1), or exploding weights all indicate problems.</li><li><strong>Check data pipeline</strong>: visualise a batch before training. Ensure normalisation is correct and labels align with inputs.</li></ol>' },
    { type: 'code', lang: 'python', src: 'import torch\nimport torch.nn as nn\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\n# 1. Overfit a single batch � must-do before any real training\ndef sanity_overfit(model, batch_x, batch_y, criterion, lr=1e-3, steps=200):\n    opt = torch.optim.Adam(model.parameters(), lr=lr)\n    model.train()\n    for step in range(steps):\n        opt.zero_grad()\n        loss = criterion(model(batch_x), batch_y)\n        loss.backward()\n        opt.step()\n        if step % 50 == 0:\n            print(f"  Step {step:3d}: loss={loss.item():.6f}")\n    print(f"Final loss: {loss.item():.8f}")\n    assert loss.item() < 1e-3, "Model FAILED to overfit one batch � check architecture"\n    print("PASS: model can memorise a single batch")\n\n# 2. Gradient flow check\ndef check_gradient_flow(model):\n    print("\\nGradient flow:")\n    no_grad, small_grad, ok_grad = [], [], []\n    for name, param in model.named_parameters():\n        if param.requires_grad and param.grad is not None:\n            g = param.grad.abs().mean().item()\n            if g == 0:          no_grad.append(name)\n            elif g < 1e-7:     small_grad.append((name, g))\n            else:               ok_grad.append((name, g))\n    if no_grad:    print(f"  DEAD (grad=0): {no_grad}")\n    if small_grad: print(f"  VANISHING:     {[(n, f\'{g:.2e}\') for n, g in small_grad]}")\n    print(f"  OK:            {[n for n, _ in ok_grad]}")' },
    { type: 'code', lang: 'python', src: '# 3. Track activation statistics per layer\ndef register_activation_hooks(model):\n    stats = {}\n    hooks = []\n    def make_hook(name):\n        def hook(module, input, output):\n            stats[name] = {\n                "mean":    output.mean().item(),\n                "std":     output.std().item(),\n                "frac_zero": (output == 0).float().mean().item()\n            }\n        return hook\n    for name, module in model.named_modules():\n        if isinstance(module, (nn.ReLU, nn.Linear, nn.BatchNorm1d)):\n            hooks.append(module.register_forward_hook(make_hook(name)))\n    return stats, hooks\n\n# Usage:\n# stats, hooks = register_activation_hooks(model)\n# _ = model(x)  # forward pass\n# for layer, s in stats.items():\n#     print(f"{layer:30s} | mean={s[\'mean\']:+.3f} std={s[\'std\']:.3f} dead={s[\'frac_zero\']:.1%}")\n# for h in hooks: h.remove()  # always clean up hooks\n\n# 4. Common NaN causes\nprint("NaN debugging checklist:")\nprint("  ? Learning rate too high � reduce by 10x")\nprint("  ? No gradient clipping with large LR or RNNs")\nprint("  ? log(0) in loss � add epsilon or use log_softmax")\nprint("  ? Division by zero in custom layer")\nprint("  ? Incorrect normalisation � large input scale")\nprint("  ? FP16 underflow � check GradScaler config")' },
    { type: 'tip', title: 'Use torch.autograd.detect_anomaly()', body: 'Wrap your forward+backward pass in `with torch.autograd.detect_anomaly():` during debugging � it will raise an exception at the exact operation that produced a NaN or Inf and print the traceback to that operation. Never leave it on during production training (10x slower).' }
  ]
};

L['dl-w2-l5'] = {
  duration_mins: 55,
  sections: [
    { type: 'text', body: '<h2>Learning Rate Scheduling & Advanced Training Tricks</h2><p>The learning rate should not be constant throughout training. Modern training recipes use <strong>schedules</strong> that warm up, maintain a peak, then decay � extracting the last few percentage points of accuracy that separate good models from great ones.</p>' },
    { type: 'code', lang: 'python', src: 'import torch\nimport torch.nn as nn\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Visualise LR schedules\ndef plot_schedule(scheduler_name, scheduler, steps=500):\n    lrs = []\n    for _ in range(steps):\n        lrs.append(scheduler.get_last_lr()[0])\n        scheduler.step()\n    return lrs\n\nmodel  = nn.Linear(1, 1)\nfig, axes = plt.subplots(2, 2, figsize=(12, 8))\n\n# 1. Cosine Annealing\nopt = torch.optim.SGD(model.parameters(), lr=0.1)\nsch = torch.optim.lr_scheduler.CosineAnnealingLR(opt, T_max=500, eta_min=1e-6)\nlrs = plot_schedule("CosineAnnealing", sch)\naxes[0,0].plot(lrs); axes[0,0].set_title("Cosine Annealing")\n\n# 2. OneCycleLR (warmup + cosine decay)\nopt2 = torch.optim.AdamW(model.parameters(), lr=1e-4)\nsch2 = torch.optim.lr_scheduler.OneCycleLR(opt2, max_lr=0.1,\n                                            total_steps=500, pct_start=0.3)\nlrs2 = []\nfor _ in range(500):\n    lrs2.append(opt2.param_groups[0]["lr"]); sch2.step()\naxes[0,1].plot(lrs2); axes[0,1].set_title("OneCycleLR (warmup + decay)")\n\n# 3. ReduceLROnPlateau (adaptive)\nopt3 = torch.optim.Adam(model.parameters(), lr=0.1)\nsch3 = torch.optim.lr_scheduler.ReduceLROnPlateau(opt3, patience=20, factor=0.5)\nlrs3 = []\nfor i in range(500):\n    # Simulate a val_loss that stops improving at step 100\n    fake_loss = max(0.1, 1.0 - i*0.008 if i < 100 else 0.2 + (i-100)*0.0002)\n    sch3.step(fake_loss); lrs3.append(opt3.param_groups[0]["lr"])\naxes[1,0].plot(lrs3); axes[1,0].set_title("ReduceLROnPlateau")\n\n# 4. Warmup + Cosine (custom for transformers)\ndef warmup_cosine(step, warmup_steps=50, total_steps=500, min_lr=1e-6, max_lr=3e-4):\n    if step < warmup_steps:\n        return max_lr * step / warmup_steps\n    progress = (step - warmup_steps) / (total_steps - warmup_steps)\n    return min_lr + 0.5*(max_lr - min_lr)*(1 + np.cos(np.pi * progress))\n\nsteps = range(500)\naxes[1,1].plot([warmup_cosine(s) for s in steps])\naxes[1,1].set_title("Linear Warmup + Cosine Decay (transformers)")\n\nfor ax in axes.flat:\n    ax.set_xlabel("Step"); ax.set_ylabel("LR")\nplt.suptitle("Learning Rate Schedules"); plt.tight_layout(); plt.show()' },
    { type: 'text', body: '<h3>Label Smoothing</h3><p>Hard labels (0 or 1) push the network to produce infinite logits � impossible and numerically unstable. Label smoothing replaces hard targets with soft ones: y_smooth = (1-e)�y_hard + e/K where K is number of classes, e=0.1 is typical. Effects: reduces overconfidence, improves calibration (predicted probabilities more closely match true frequencies), often improves accuracy by 0.3�0.5%.</p>' },
    { type: 'code', lang: 'python', src: 'import torch\nimport torch.nn as nn\n\n# Label smoothing is built into CrossEntropyLoss in PyTorch >= 1.10\ncriterion = nn.CrossEntropyLoss(label_smoothing=0.1)\n\n# Or manually\nclass LabelSmoothingLoss(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, classes, smoothing=0.1):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.smoothing = smoothing\n        self.cls = classes\n    def forward(self, pred, target):\n        smooth_target = torch.full_like(pred, self.smoothing / (self.cls - 1))\n        smooth_target.scatter_(1, target.unsqueeze(1), 1.0 - self.smoothing)\n        return nn.functional.cross_entropy(pred, smooth_target.argmax(1))\n\n# Mixup augmentation � interpolate between pairs of training examples\ndef mixup_batch(x, y, alpha=0.2):\n    """Returns mixed inputs and a pair of labels with interpolation weight."""\n    lam = torch.distributions.Beta(alpha, alpha).sample().item()\n    idx = torch.randperm(x.size(0))\n    mixed_x = lam * x + (1 - lam) * x[idx]\n    y_a, y_b = y, y[idx]\n    return mixed_x, y_a, y_b, lam\n\ndef mixup_criterion(criterion, pred, y_a, y_b, lam):\n    return lam * criterion(pred, y_a) + (1 - lam) * criterion(pred, y_b)' },
    { type: 'tip', title: 'Gradient accumulation for large effective batch sizes', body: 'If your GPU OOM with batch size 64 but you want effective batch 256, accumulate gradients over 4 steps: call loss.backward() each step but only call optimiser.step() and zero_grad() every 4 steps. Scale the loss by 1/accumulation_steps to keep gradient magnitudes equivalent.' }
  ]
};

