L['dl-w4-l1'] = {
  duration_mins: 50,
  sections: [
    { type: 'text', body: '<h2>Recurrent Neural Networks & LSTMs</h2><p>Standard feedforward networks process fixed-size inputs independently � each input is unrelated to the next. But language, time series, audio, and video are <strong>sequences</strong>: meaning depends on order, and context from earlier steps matters. <strong>Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs)</strong> process sequences step by step, maintaining a hidden state that carries information across timesteps.</p>' },
    { type: 'text', body: '<h3>The Vanishing Gradient in RNNs</h3><p>At timestep t, the RNN computes: h? = tanh(W?x? + W?h??1 + b). Through backpropagation-through-time (BPTT), gradients flow backwards through every timestep. After 50 steps, the gradient is multiplied by W? 50 times � if |W?| &lt; 1, it vanishes to zero; if |W?| &gt; 1, it explodes. Plain RNNs cannot learn dependencies longer than ~10-20 timesteps in practice.</p>' },
    { type: 'text', body: '<h3>Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM)</h3><p>Hochreiter & Schmidhuber (1997) solved the vanishing gradient problem with the <strong>cell state</strong> � a separate memory pathway that can store information for hundreds of timesteps with only additive (not multiplicative) updates. Three gates control information flow:</p><ul><li><strong>Forget gate</strong> f?: what to erase from cell state</li><li><strong>Input gate</strong> i?: what new information to write</li><li><strong>Output gate</strong> o?: what to expose as hidden state h?</li></ul><p>Cell state update: C? = f? ? C??1 + i? ? C~? � only element-wise multiplication and addition, so gradients flow unimpeded.</p>' },
    { type: 'code', lang: 'python', src: 'import torch\nimport torch.nn as nn\n\n# PyTorch LSTM � stack multiple layers\nlstm = nn.LSTM(\n    input_size=64,   # embedding dimension\n    hidden_size=256, # hidden state size\n    num_layers=2,    # stacked layers\n    batch_first=True,# input shape: (batch, seq_len, features)\n    dropout=0.3,     # dropout between layers (not on last layer)\n    bidirectional=False\n)\n\n# Forward pass\nbatch_size, seq_len, input_dim = 32, 100, 64\nx = torch.randn(batch_size, seq_len, input_dim)\nh0 = torch.zeros(2, batch_size, 256)  # (num_layers, batch, hidden)\nc0 = torch.zeros(2, batch_size, 256)\noutput, (hn, cn) = lstm(x, (h0, c0))\nprint(f"Output:  {output.shape}")  # (32, 100, 256) � all timestep outputs\nprint(f"hn:      {hn.shape}")      # (2, 32, 256) � final hidden state\nprint(f"cn:      {cn.shape}")      # (2, 32, 256) � final cell state\n\n# Sentiment classifier using LSTM\nclass SentimentLSTM(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, vocab_size, embed_dim=128, hidden_dim=256, num_layers=2):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.embedding = nn.Embedding(vocab_size, embed_dim, padding_idx=0)\n        self.lstm = nn.LSTM(embed_dim, hidden_dim, num_layers,\n                            batch_first=True, dropout=0.3, bidirectional=True)\n        self.classifier = nn.Sequential(\n            nn.Dropout(0.4),\n            nn.Linear(hidden_dim * 2, 64),  # *2 for bidirectional\n            nn.ReLU(),\n            nn.Linear(64, 1)  # binary sentiment\n        )\n    def forward(self, x):\n        embedded = self.embedding(x)                # (B, T, E)\n        out, _   = self.lstm(embedded)              # (B, T, H*2)\n        pooled   = out.mean(dim=1)                  # mean pool over time\n        return self.classifier(pooled)' },
    { type: 'text', body: '<h3>GRU � Gated Recurrent Unit</h3><p>A simplified LSTM (Cho et al., 2014) with just two gates: reset and update. No separate cell state. Fewer parameters than LSTM � similar performance on many tasks, trains slightly faster. A good default when LSTM feels like overkill.</p>' },
    { type: 'tip', title: 'Bidirectional RNNs for encoding tasks', body: 'A bidirectional LSTM processes the sequence forwards and backwards simultaneously, giving each timestep context from both past and future. Ideal for classification, NER, and sequence-to-vector tasks. Cannot be used for generation/forecasting (future is unknown at inference time).' }
  ]
};

L['dl-w4-l2'] = {
  duration_mins: 55,
  sections: [
    { type: 'text', body: '<h2>Sequence-to-Sequence Models & Attention</h2><p>RNNs encode a sequence into a fixed-size vector. For tasks that require generating variable-length outputs (translation, summarisation, speech recognition), this bottleneck becomes a severe limitation � all information must pass through a single vector regardless of input length. The <strong>sequence-to-sequence</strong> (seq2seq) architecture with an <strong>attention mechanism</strong> solved this by letting the decoder selectively focus on different parts of the input at each generation step.</p>' },
    { type: 'text', body: '<h3>Encoder-Decoder Architecture</h3><p>The encoder processes the full input sequence, producing a hidden state at every timestep. The decoder generates the output one token at a time. At each decoder step, <strong>attention</strong> computes a weighted average over all encoder hidden states � the weights (attention scores) determine which input positions are most relevant for generating the current output token.</p><p>The attention score between decoder query q and encoder key k: score(q,k) = q?k / vd?. Softmax normalises scores to probabilities (attention weights). Context vector = S weight � encoder_hidden_state.</p>' },
    { type: 'code', lang: 'python', src: 'import torch\nimport torch.nn as nn\nimport torch.nn.functional as F\n\nclass BahdanauAttention(nn.Module):\n    """Additive attention (Bahdanau et al. 2015) � the original seq2seq attention."""\n    def __init__(self, hidden_dim):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.Wa = nn.Linear(hidden_dim, hidden_dim)  # decoder query\n        self.Ua = nn.Linear(hidden_dim, hidden_dim)  # encoder keys\n        self.Va = nn.Linear(hidden_dim, 1)           # energy scalar\n\n    def forward(self, query, encoder_outputs):\n        """\n        query:           (B, H) � decoder hidden state at current step\n        encoder_outputs: (B, T, H) � all encoder hidden states\n        returns: context (B, H), attn_weights (B, T)\n        """\n        query_exp = query.unsqueeze(1).expand_as(encoder_outputs)  # (B, T, H)\n        energy = self.Va(torch.tanh(self.Wa(query_exp) + self.Ua(encoder_outputs)))\n        attn_weights = F.softmax(energy.squeeze(-1), dim=1)        # (B, T)\n        context = (attn_weights.unsqueeze(2) * encoder_outputs).sum(dim=1)  # (B, H)\n        return context, attn_weights\n\nclass Seq2SeqDecoder(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, vocab_size, embed_dim, hidden_dim):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.embedding = nn.Embedding(vocab_size, embed_dim)\n        self.attention  = BahdanauAttention(hidden_dim)\n        self.gru        = nn.GRU(embed_dim + hidden_dim, hidden_dim, batch_first=True)\n        self.out        = nn.Linear(hidden_dim, vocab_size)\n\n    def forward(self, token, hidden, encoder_outputs):\n        """\n        token:  (B,) current input token\n        hidden: (1, B, H) GRU hidden state\n        encoder_outputs: (B, T, H)\n        """\n        embedded = self.embedding(token).unsqueeze(1)      # (B, 1, E)\n        context, attn = self.attention(hidden.squeeze(0), encoder_outputs)\n        rnn_input  = torch.cat([embedded, context.unsqueeze(1)], dim=2)  # (B,1,E+H)\n        output, hidden = self.gru(rnn_input, hidden)\n        logits = self.out(output.squeeze(1))\n        return logits, hidden, attn' },
    { type: 'text', body: '<h3>Visualising Attention</h3><p>One of attention\'s great advantages: interpretability. Plotting the attention weight matrix (decoder timestep � encoder timestep) shows which source words aligned with each target word during translation. In a French?English model, "chien" generating "dog" should produce a high attention weight at the "chien" position � and it does.</p>' },
    { type: 'code', lang: 'python', src: 'import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Simulate attention weights for illustration\nfr_words = ["Le", "chat", "mange", "une", "souris", "."]\nen_words = ["The", "cat", "eats", "a", "mouse", "."]\n\n# Realistic attention pattern (mostly diagonal with some alignment)\nattention = np.array([\n    [0.85, 0.05, 0.03, 0.04, 0.02, 0.01],  # The <- Le\n    [0.02, 0.90, 0.03, 0.02, 0.02, 0.01],  # cat <- chat\n    [0.01, 0.02, 0.91, 0.03, 0.02, 0.01],  # eats <- mange\n    [0.01, 0.02, 0.02, 0.88, 0.05, 0.02],  # a <- une\n    [0.01, 0.02, 0.02, 0.03, 0.90, 0.02],  # mouse <- souris\n    [0.01, 0.01, 0.01, 0.01, 0.01, 0.95],  # . <- .\n])\n\nplt.figure(figsize=(7, 6))\nplt.imshow(attention, cmap="Blues", aspect="auto")\nplt.colorbar(label="Attention weight")\nplt.xticks(range(len(fr_words)), fr_words); plt.yticks(range(len(en_words)), en_words)\nplt.xlabel("French (source)"); plt.ylabel("English (target)")\nplt.title("Alignment attention � French to English translation")\nplt.tight_layout(); plt.show()' },
    { type: 'tip', title: 'Teacher forcing � training seq2seq models', body: 'During training, feed the ground-truth previous token as input to the decoder (not the predicted token). This is called teacher forcing and makes training stable. At inference, use the model\'s own predictions. Scheduled sampling gradually transitions from teacher forcing to self-feeding during training to prevent train/test mismatch.' }
  ]
};

L['dl-w4-l3'] = {
  duration_mins: 50,
  sections: [
    { type: 'text', body: '<h2>The Transformer Architecture</h2><p>"Attention is All You Need" (Vaswani et al., 2017) is arguably the most impactful paper in modern AI. It replaced the recurrent architecture of seq2seq models entirely with <strong>self-attention</strong> � allowing the model to relate any position in the sequence to any other position in a single step, with no sequential dependency. Transformers enabled massive parallelism during training, leading directly to GPT, BERT, and the large language model revolution.</p>' },
    { type: 'text', body: '<h3>Self-Attention � The Core Operation</h3><p>For each token, compute three projections: Query (Q), Key (K), Value (V) from learned weight matrices. The attention score of token i attending to token j is:</p><p style="text-align:center;padding:.5rem;background:var(--fog);border-radius:6px;">Attention(Q,K,V) = softmax(QK? / vd?) � V</p><p>The dot product QK? measures compatibility between query and key � how much token i should attend to token j. Scaling by vd? prevents the dot product from growing too large for large dimensions. The result: a weighted sum of Value vectors, where weights reflect relevance.</p>' },
    { type: 'code', lang: 'python', src: 'import torch\nimport torch.nn as nn\nimport torch.nn.functional as F\nimport math\n\nclass MultiHeadAttention(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, d_model, num_heads, dropout=0.1):\n        super().__init__()\n        assert d_model % num_heads == 0\n        self.d_model   = d_model\n        self.num_heads = num_heads\n        self.d_k       = d_model // num_heads\n        self.Wq = nn.Linear(d_model, d_model)\n        self.Wk = nn.Linear(d_model, d_model)\n        self.Wv = nn.Linear(d_model, d_model)\n        self.Wo = nn.Linear(d_model, d_model)\n        self.dropout = nn.Dropout(dropout)\n\n    def split_heads(self, x, B):\n        """(B, T, d_model) -> (B, num_heads, T, d_k)"""\n        return x.view(B, -1, self.num_heads, self.d_k).transpose(1, 2)\n\n    def forward(self, query, key, value, mask=None):\n        B = query.size(0)\n        Q = self.split_heads(self.Wq(query), B)\n        K = self.split_heads(self.Wk(key),   B)\n        V = self.split_heads(self.Wv(value), B)\n        # Scaled dot-product attention\n        scores = Q @ K.transpose(-2,-1) / math.sqrt(self.d_k)\n        if mask is not None:\n            scores = scores.masked_fill(mask == 0, -1e9)\n        attn   = self.dropout(F.softmax(scores, dim=-1))\n        out    = (attn @ V).transpose(1,2).contiguous().view(B, -1, self.d_model)\n        return self.Wo(out), attn' },
    { type: 'code', lang: 'python', src: '# Full Transformer encoder block\nclass TransformerBlock(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, d_model=256, num_heads=8, ff_dim=1024, dropout=0.1):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.attn    = MultiHeadAttention(d_model, num_heads, dropout)\n        self.ff      = nn.Sequential(\n            nn.Linear(d_model, ff_dim),\n            nn.GELU(),\n            nn.Dropout(dropout),\n            nn.Linear(ff_dim, d_model)\n        )\n        self.norm1   = nn.LayerNorm(d_model)\n        self.norm2   = nn.LayerNorm(d_model)\n        self.dropout = nn.Dropout(dropout)\n\n    def forward(self, x, mask=None):\n        # Pre-LayerNorm (modern variant � more stable than original post-LN)\n        attn_out, _  = self.attn(self.norm1(x), self.norm1(x), self.norm1(x), mask)\n        x = x + self.dropout(attn_out)  # residual connection\n        ff_out = self.ff(self.norm2(x))\n        x = x + self.dropout(ff_out)    # residual connection\n        return x\n\n# Positional encoding � injects token position since attention is permutation-invariant\nclass PositionalEncoding(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, d_model, max_len=512, dropout=0.1):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.dropout = nn.Dropout(dropout)\n        pe = torch.zeros(max_len, d_model)\n        pos = torch.arange(0, max_len).unsqueeze(1)\n        div = torch.exp(torch.arange(0, d_model, 2) * (-math.log(10000) / d_model))\n        pe[:, 0::2] = torch.sin(pos * div)\n        pe[:, 1::2] = torch.cos(pos * div)\n        self.register_buffer("pe", pe.unsqueeze(0))\n\n    def forward(self, x):\n        return self.dropout(x + self.pe[:, :x.size(1)])\n\n# Test\nblock = TransformerBlock(d_model=256, num_heads=8)\nx = torch.randn(4, 50, 256)  # (batch, seq_len, d_model)\nprint("Transformer block output:", block(x).shape)' },
    { type: 'tip', title: 'Multi-head attention � why multiple heads?', body: 'Each attention head can learn to attend to different aspects: one head might capture syntactic dependencies, another semantic similarity, another co-reference. Multiple heads in parallel then combine their perspectives. 8 heads with d_model=512 means each head has d_k=64 � a natural split that works well in practice.' }
  ]
};

L['dl-w4-l4'] = {
  duration_mins: 50,
  sections: [
    { type: 'text', body: '<h2>BERT � Pre-training & Fine-Tuning Transformers</h2><p>BERT (Devlin et al., 2019) applied the transformer encoder to NLP with a critical insight: train on massive unlabelled text using self-supervised objectives, then fine-tune on labelled downstream tasks with very few examples. This two-phase paradigm � <strong>pretrain then fine-tune</strong> � became the dominant approach in NLP and later in vision, audio, and multimodal AI.</p>' },
    { type: 'text', body: '<h3>BERT Pre-training Objectives</h3><p><strong>Masked Language Modelling (MLM)</strong>: randomly mask 15% of tokens; predict the masked tokens using surrounding context. Forces the model to understand bidirectional context � unlike GPT which only sees left context.</p><p><strong>Next Sentence Prediction (NSP)</strong>: given two sentences A and B, predict if B follows A in the document. Later research (RoBERTa) showed NSP hurts more than it helps � removed in subsequent models.</p><p>BERT-base: 12 transformer layers, 12 attention heads, d_model=768, 110M parameters. BERT-large: 24 layers, 16 heads, d_model=1024, 340M parameters.</p>' },
    { type: 'code', lang: 'python', src: '# Fine-tune BERT for sentiment classification using HuggingFace transformers\nfrom transformers import BertTokenizer, BertForSequenceClassification\nfrom transformers import Trainer, TrainingArguments\nfrom datasets import Dataset\nimport torch\n\n# 1. Load pretrained tokeniser and model\ntokeniser = BertTokenizer.from_pretrained("bert-base-uncased")\nmodel     = BertForSequenceClassification.from_pretrained(\n    "bert-base-uncased", num_labels=2  # positive / negative\n)\n\n# 2. Tokenise dataset\ntexts  = ["This movie is brilliant!", "Terrible film, waste of time.",\n           "Outstanding performance!", "Boring and predictable."]\nlabels = [1, 0, 1, 0]\n\ndef tokenise(examples):\n    return tokeniser(examples["text"], truncation=True, padding="max_length",\n                     max_length=128, return_tensors="pt")\n\ndataset = Dataset.from_dict({"text": texts, "label": labels})\ntokenised = dataset.map(lambda x: tokeniser(x["text"], truncation=True,\n                                            padding="max_length", max_length=128),\n                        batched=True)\ntokenised = tokenised.rename_column("label", "labels")  # Trainer expects "labels"\n\n# 3. Training arguments\nargs = TrainingArguments(\n    output_dir="bert-sentiment",\n    num_train_epochs=3,\n    per_device_train_batch_size=8,\n    per_device_eval_batch_size=16,\n    learning_rate=2e-5,\n    weight_decay=0.01,\n    warmup_ratio=0.1,\n    evaluation_strategy="epoch",\n    save_strategy="epoch",\n    load_best_model_at_end=True,\n    fp16=torch.cuda.is_available(),\n    report_to="none"\n)\n\ntrainer = Trainer(\n    model=model, args=args,\n    train_dataset=tokenised, eval_dataset=tokenised\n)\n# trainer.train()  # uncomment to actually train' },
    { type: 'code', lang: 'python', src: '# BERT zero-shot inference without fine-tuning (using pipeline)\nfrom transformers import pipeline\n\n# Sentiment � fine-tuned on SST-2\nsentiment = pipeline("sentiment-analysis", device=-1)  # -1 = CPU\nresults = sentiment(["This course is fantastic!", "I struggled to follow the content."])\nfor r in results:\n    print(f"{r[\'label\']:10s} ({r[\'score\']:.3f})")\n\n# Named Entity Recognition\nner = pipeline("ner", aggregation_strategy="simple", device=-1)\ntext = "Sundar Pichai leads Google, based in Mountain View, California."\nentities = ner(text)\nfor e in entities:\n    print(f"{e[\'word\']:20s} -> {e[\'entity_group\']} ({e[\'score\']:.3f})")\n\n# Fill-mask (MLM inference)\nfill = pipeline("fill-mask", model="bert-base-uncased", device=-1)\nresult = fill("Data science is the [MASK] of the 21st century.")\nfor r in result[:3]:\n    print(f"score={r[\'score\']:.3f}  token={r[\'token_str\']:<15s}  sentence={r[\'sequence\']}")' },
    { type: 'tip', title: 'Always use a small LR for fine-tuning BERT', body: 'Pretrained transformer weights are fragile � aggressive learning rates destroy them in the first few steps (catastrophic forgetting). Use lr=2e-5 to 5e-5 for full fine-tuning, with warmup for the first 6-10% of steps and cosine decay. This is the recipe from the original BERT paper and it still works.' }
  ]
};

L['dl-w4-l5'] = {
  duration_mins: 55,
  sections: [
    { type: 'text', body: '<h2>Time Series & Forecasting with Deep Learning</h2><p>Time series forecasting � predicting future values from past observations � is one of the highest-value applications of deep learning in industry: energy load forecasting, stock price prediction, demand forecasting, anomaly detection in sensor data. Deep models (LSTM, Temporal CNN, Transformer) can capture complex non-linear temporal patterns that classical ARIMA models cannot.</p>' },
    { type: 'text', body: '<h3>Temporal Convolutional Networks (TCN)</h3><p>An alternative to LSTMs for sequences: apply 1D convolutions with <strong>dilated causal convolutions</strong>. Causal = the output at time t depends only on t and earlier inputs (no future leakage). Dilation = exponentially increasing receptive field (1, 2, 4, 8, �) without increasing parameters. TCNs train faster than LSTMs, are easier to parallise, and often match or beat LSTMs on time series.</p>' },
    { type: 'code', lang: 'python', src: 'import torch\nimport torch.nn as nn\nimport numpy as np\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\n# Dilated causal convolution block\nclass CausalConvBlock(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, in_ch, out_ch, kernel_size=3, dilation=1, dropout=0.2):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.padding = (kernel_size - 1) * dilation\n        self.conv = nn.Conv1d(in_ch, out_ch, kernel_size,\n                              padding=self.padding, dilation=dilation)\n        self.norm    = nn.BatchNorm1d(out_ch)\n        self.dropout = nn.Dropout(dropout)\n        self.relu    = nn.ReLU()\n        self.residual = nn.Conv1d(in_ch, out_ch, 1) if in_ch != out_ch else nn.Identity()\n\n    def forward(self, x):\n        out = self.conv(x)[:, :, :-self.padding]  # trim to causal length\n        out = self.relu(self.norm(out))\n        out = self.dropout(out)\n        return out + self.residual(x)\n\nclass TCN(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, input_size=1, output_size=1, channels=[32,64,64], kernel_size=3):\n        super().__init__()\n        layers = []\n        in_ch = input_size\n        for i, out_ch in enumerate(channels):\n            layers.append(CausalConvBlock(in_ch, out_ch, kernel_size, dilation=2**i))\n            in_ch = out_ch\n        self.network = nn.Sequential(*layers)\n        self.head = nn.Linear(channels[-1], output_size)\n\n    def forward(self, x):\n        # x: (batch, seq_len, features) -> (batch, features, seq_len)\n        out = self.network(x.transpose(1,2))\n        return self.head(out[:, :, -1])  # use last timestep for prediction' },
    { type: 'code', lang: 'python', src: '# Complete forecasting pipeline � electricity demand example\ndef generate_demand_data(n=2000):\n    t = np.linspace(0, 20*np.pi, n)\n    trend    = 0.05 * t\n    seasonal = 3 * np.sin(t) + 1.5 * np.sin(2*t)\n    noise    = np.random.randn(n) * 0.5\n    return (trend + seasonal + noise).astype(np.float32)\n\ndef create_sequences(data, seq_len=48, horizon=12):\n    X, y = [], []\n    for i in range(len(data) - seq_len - horizon + 1):\n        X.append(data[i : i+seq_len])\n        y.append(data[i+seq_len : i+seq_len+horizon])\n    return np.array(X), np.array(y)\n\ndata = generate_demand_data(3000)\n# Normalise\nmean, std = data[:2000].mean(), data[:2000].std()\ndata_norm = (data - mean) / std\n\nX, y = create_sequences(data_norm, seq_len=96, horizon=24)\nX = X[..., np.newaxis]  # add feature dim\n\nsplit = int(len(X) * 0.8)\nX_tr, y_tr = torch.tensor(X[:split]), torch.tensor(y[:split])\nX_va, y_va = torch.tensor(X[split:]), torch.tensor(y[split:])\n\nmodel = TCN(input_size=1, output_size=24, channels=[32,64,128])\noptimiser = torch.optim.AdamW(model.parameters(), lr=3e-4)\ncriterion = nn.HuberLoss()\n\nfor epoch in range(50):\n    model.train(); optimiser.zero_grad()\n    pred = model(X_tr)\n    loss = criterion(pred, y_tr)\n    loss.backward(); optimiser.step()\n    if (epoch+1) % 10 == 0:\n        model.eval()\n        with torch.no_grad(): val_loss = criterion(model(X_va), y_va)\n        print(f"Ep {epoch+1:2d} train={loss.item():.4f} val={val_loss.item():.4f}")\n\n# Denormalise and plot forecast\nmodel.eval()\nwith torch.no_grad():\n    sample_pred = model(X_va[-1:]).numpy()[0] * std + mean\nsample_true = y_va[-1].numpy() * std + mean\nplt.figure(figsize=(10,4))\nplt.plot(sample_true, label="Actual"); plt.plot(sample_pred, label="Forecast")\nplt.title("24-step Electricity Demand Forecast"); plt.legend(); plt.show()' },
    { type: 'tip', title: 'Temporal Fusion Transformer (TFT)', body: 'For production forecasting, the Temporal Fusion Transformer (Lim et al., 2021) is state-of-the-art: it handles static metadata, known future inputs (holidays, promotions), and unknown future inputs simultaneously, with built-in attention for interpretability. Available in the PyTorch Forecasting library.' }
  ]
};

