L['dl-w5-l1'] = {
  duration_mins: 50,
  sections: [
    { type: 'text', body: '<h2>Vision Transformers (ViT) & Modern Vision Architectures</h2><p>In 2020, Dosovitskiy et al. showed that a pure transformer applied directly to images could match or exceed CNNs � without convolutions. The Vision Transformer (ViT) treats an image as a sequence of patches and applies the transformer encoder directly. This sparked a wave of hybrid architectures (Swin Transformer, ConvNeXt) that dominate modern computer vision benchmarks.</p>' },
    { type: 'text', body: '<h3>Vision Transformer (ViT) Architecture</h3><p>ViT divides a 224�224 image into 16�16 patches (14�14=196 patches). Each patch is linearly projected to a d_model-dimensional vector (patch embedding). A learnable <code>[CLS]</code> token is prepended. Sinusoidal or learned positional embeddings are added. The sequence is processed by standard transformer encoder blocks. The <code>[CLS]</code> token output goes to the classification head.</p><p>Key insight: with enough pretraining data, ViT outperforms all CNNs. With limited data, inductive biases of CNNs (local connectivity, translation equivariance) win.</p>' },
    { type: 'code', lang: 'python', src: 'import torch\nimport torch.nn as nn\n\nclass PatchEmbedding(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, img_size=224, patch_size=16, in_ch=3, d_model=768):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.n_patches = (img_size // patch_size) ** 2\n        # Use a Conv2d to extract patches � equivalent to flatten + linear, but faster\n        self.proj = nn.Conv2d(in_ch, d_model, kernel_size=patch_size, stride=patch_size)\n\n    def forward(self, x):\n        x = self.proj(x)                       # (B, d_model, H/P, W/P)\n        x = x.flatten(2).transpose(1, 2)       # (B, N_patches, d_model)\n        return x\n\nclass ViT(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, img_size=224, patch_size=16, in_ch=3, num_classes=1000,\n                 d_model=768, num_heads=12, num_layers=12, ff_dim=3072, dropout=0.1):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.patch_embed = PatchEmbedding(img_size, patch_size, in_ch, d_model)\n        n_patches = (img_size // patch_size) ** 2\n        self.cls_token = nn.Parameter(torch.zeros(1, 1, d_model))\n        self.pos_embed = nn.Parameter(torch.randn(1, n_patches+1, d_model) * 0.02)\n        self.dropout   = nn.Dropout(dropout)\n        encoder_layer  = nn.TransformerEncoderLayer(\n            d_model, num_heads, ff_dim, dropout, activation="gelu",\n            batch_first=True, norm_first=True  # pre-LayerNorm\n        )\n        self.transformer = nn.TransformerEncoder(encoder_layer, num_layers)\n        self.norm = nn.LayerNorm(d_model)\n        self.head = nn.Linear(d_model, num_classes)\n\n    def forward(self, x):\n        B = x.shape[0]\n        x = self.patch_embed(x)\n        cls = self.cls_token.expand(B, -1, -1)\n        x = torch.cat([cls, x], dim=1) + self.pos_embed\n        x = self.dropout(x)\n        x = self.norm(self.transformer(x))\n        return self.head(x[:, 0])  # CLS token\n\n# ViT-Base (official config)\nvit_b16 = ViT(img_size=224, patch_size=16, d_model=768, num_heads=12, num_layers=12)\nprint(f"ViT-B/16 parameters: {sum(p.numel() for p in vit_b16.parameters())/1e6:.1f}M")\nprint(f"Output shape: {vit_b16(torch.randn(2, 3, 224, 224)).shape}")' },
    { type: 'code', lang: 'python', src: '# Use pretrained ViT from torchvision (most practical approach)\nimport torchvision.models as models\n\n# ViT-B/16 pretrained on ImageNet-21k then fine-tuned on ImageNet-1k\nvit = models.vit_b_16(weights=models.ViT_B_16_Weights.IMAGENET1K_V1)\n\n# Swin Transformer � hierarchical ViT with sliding window attention (CNN-like hierarchy)\nswin = models.swin_t(weights=models.Swin_T_Weights.IMAGENET1K_V1)\n\n# ConvNeXt � CNN redesigned with transformer training tricks (VERY strong baseline)\nconvnext = models.convnext_tiny(weights=models.ConvNeXt_Tiny_Weights.IMAGENET1K_V1)\n\n# Benchmark sizes\nfor name, m in [("ViT-B/16", vit), ("Swin-T", swin), ("ConvNeXt-T", convnext)]:\n    params = sum(p.numel() for p in m.parameters())\n    print(f"{name:15s} {params/1e6:.1f}M params")' },
    { type: 'tip', title: 'EfficientNetV2 or ConvNeXt for practical use', body: 'For most production image classification tasks, EfficientNetV2-S or ConvNeXt-Tiny hit the best accuracy/speed tradeoff. ViT requires large datasets or very large pretraining � its advantage is most pronounced at scale (>100M parameters, billions of training images). For standard fine-tuning tasks, ConvNeXt with a simple recipe typically wins.' }
  ]
};

L['dl-w5-l2'] = {
  duration_mins: 50,
  sections: [
    { type: 'text', body: '<h2>Generative Models � VAEs & GANs</h2><p>Generative models learn the data distribution P(X) and can <em>sample new data</em> from it. They power image synthesis, style transfer, data augmentation, drug discovery, and more. Two foundational architectures: <strong>Variational Autoencoders (VAEs)</strong> offer principled probabilistic generation; <strong>GANs</strong> produce sharper samples via adversarial training.</p>' },
    { type: 'text', body: '<h3>Variational Autoencoder (VAE)</h3><p>A standard autoencoder compresses input to a latent vector z and reconstructs it. VAEs make the latent space probabilistic: the encoder outputs a distribution (mean � and variance s�) rather than a point. The decoder samples from this distribution. The loss combines reconstruction loss (fidelity) and KL divergence (regularise latent space to be Gaussian):</p><p style="text-align:center;padding:.5rem;background:var(--fog);border-radius:6px;">L = E[log P(x|z)] - � � KL[q(z|x) || N(0,I)]</p><p>The reparameterisation trick makes sampling differentiable: z = � + s � e, e ~ N(0,I).</p>' },
    { type: 'code', lang: 'python', src: 'import torch\nimport torch.nn as nn\nimport torch.nn.functional as F\n\nclass VAE(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, input_dim=784, latent_dim=20, hidden_dim=400):\n        super().__init__()\n        # Encoder\n        self.fc_enc  = nn.Linear(input_dim, hidden_dim)\n        self.fc_mu   = nn.Linear(hidden_dim, latent_dim)\n        self.fc_logv = nn.Linear(hidden_dim, latent_dim)\n        # Decoder\n        self.fc_dec  = nn.Linear(latent_dim, hidden_dim)\n        self.fc_out  = nn.Linear(hidden_dim, input_dim)\n\n    def encode(self, x):\n        h    = F.relu(self.fc_enc(x))\n        mu   = self.fc_mu(h)\n        logv = self.fc_logv(h)\n        return mu, logv\n\n    def reparameterise(self, mu, logv):\n        std = torch.exp(0.5 * logv)\n        eps = torch.randn_like(std)   # sample noise\n        return mu + eps * std         # differentiable sample\n\n    def decode(self, z):\n        h = F.relu(self.fc_dec(z))\n        return torch.sigmoid(self.fc_out(h))\n\n    def forward(self, x):\n        x    = x.flatten(1)                    # (B, 784)\n        mu, logv = self.encode(x)\n        z    = self.reparameterise(mu, logv)\n        recon = self.decode(z)\n        return recon, mu, logv\n\ndef vae_loss(recon, x, mu, logv, beta=1.0):\n    recon_loss = F.binary_cross_entropy(recon, x.flatten(1), reduction="sum")\n    kl_loss = -0.5 * torch.sum(1 + logv - mu**2 - logv.exp())\n    return recon_loss + beta * kl_loss' },
    { type: 'text', body: '<h3>Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs)</h3><p>Two networks compete: a <strong>Generator</strong> G tries to create realistic fake samples; a <strong>Discriminator</strong> D tries to distinguish real from fake. They play a minimax game:</p><p style="text-align:center;padding:.5rem;background:var(--fog);border-radius:6px;">min_G max_D E[log D(x)] + E[log(1 - D(G(z)))]</p><p>At convergence, G produces samples indistinguishable from real data and D outputs 0.5 (50/50 chance). GANs produce sharper images than VAEs but are harder to train (mode collapse, training instability).</p>' },
    { type: 'code', lang: 'python', src: 'import torch\nimport torch.nn as nn\n\nclass Generator(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, latent_dim=100, img_dim=784):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.net = nn.Sequential(\n            nn.Linear(latent_dim, 256), nn.LeakyReLU(0.2),\n            nn.Linear(256, 512),        nn.LeakyReLU(0.2),\n            nn.Linear(512, img_dim),    nn.Tanh()  # output in [-1,1]\n        )\n    def forward(self, z): return self.net(z)\n\nclass Discriminator(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, img_dim=784):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.net = nn.Sequential(\n            nn.Linear(img_dim, 512),  nn.LeakyReLU(0.2), nn.Dropout(0.3),\n            nn.Linear(512, 256),      nn.LeakyReLU(0.2), nn.Dropout(0.3),\n            nn.Linear(256, 1)         # logit (no sigmoid � use BCEWithLogitsLoss)\n        )\n    def forward(self, x): return self.net(x)\n\n# GAN training step\ndef gan_step(G, D, real_imgs, latent_dim, opt_G, opt_D, criterion):\n    B = real_imgs.size(0)\n    real = real_imgs.view(B, -1) * 2 - 1  # [0,1] -> [-1,1]\n    z    = torch.randn(B, latent_dim)\n\n    # Train Discriminator\n    opt_D.zero_grad()\n    d_real = D(real.detach()); d_fake = D(G(z).detach())\n    d_loss = (criterion(d_real, torch.ones(B,1)) +\n              criterion(d_fake, torch.zeros(B,1)))\n    d_loss.backward(); opt_D.step()\n\n    # Train Generator (wants D to output 1 for fake)\n    opt_G.zero_grad()\n    fake   = G(torch.randn(B, latent_dim))\n    g_loss = criterion(D(fake), torch.ones(B,1))\n    g_loss.backward(); opt_G.step()\n    return d_loss.item(), g_loss.item()' },
    { type: 'tip', title: 'Mode collapse and how to fight it', body: 'Mode collapse: the generator learns to produce a few high-quality samples and ignores most of the data distribution (all generated faces look the same). Mitigations: use WGAN-GP (Wasserstein loss + gradient penalty) instead of vanilla GAN loss; use minibatch discrimination; increase discriminator capacity. Diffusion models have largely replaced GANs for image generation precisely because they avoid mode collapse.' }
  ]
};

L['dl-w5-l3'] = {
  duration_mins: 50,
  sections: [
    { type: 'text', body: '<h2>Diffusion Models � How Stable Diffusion Works</h2><p>Diffusion models (Ho et al., 2020; DALL-E 2; Stable Diffusion; Imagen) are now the dominant paradigm for image generation, surpassing GANs in quality, diversity, and controllability. They work by learning to reverse a gradual noising process � like teaching a network to "un-noise" an image step by step.</p>' },
    { type: 'text', body: '<h3>The Forward Process � Adding Noise</h3><p>Given a clean image x0, the forward process gradually adds Gaussian noise over T timesteps (typically T=1000). At each step t, x_t = v(??)�x0 + v(1-??)�e where e ~ N(0,I) and ?? is a noise schedule. At t=T, x_T � N(0,I) � pure noise. This process destroys all structure.</p><h3>The Reverse Process � Denoising</h3><p>The model e_? learns to predict the noise added at each step, given the noisy image and the timestep t. Training objective: predict e from x_t: L = ||e - e_?(x_t, t)||�. Sampling: start from pure noise x_T and iteratively denoise � running the model T times to produce x0.</p>' },
    { type: 'code', lang: 'python', src: '# Minimal DDPM implementation for educational purposes\nimport torch\nimport torch.nn as nn\nimport numpy as np\n\nclass NoiseSchedule:\n    def __init__(self, T=1000, beta_start=1e-4, beta_end=0.02):\n        self.T    = T\n        self.beta = torch.linspace(beta_start, beta_end, T)\n        self.alpha     = 1 - self.beta\n        self.alpha_bar = torch.cumprod(self.alpha, dim=0)  # cumulative product\n\n    def q_sample(self, x0, t):\n        """Forward process: add noise to x0 at timestep t."""\n        ab = self.alpha_bar[t].view(-1, 1, 1, 1).to(x0.device)\n        noise = torch.randn_like(x0)\n        return ab.sqrt() * x0 + (1 - ab).sqrt() * noise, noise\n\n    def p_sample(self, model, x_t, t):\n        """One reverse step: denoise x_t to x_{t-1}."""\n        beta     = self.beta[t]\n        ab       = self.alpha_bar[t]\n        ab_prev  = self.alpha_bar[t-1] if t > 0 else torch.tensor(1.0)\n        with torch.no_grad():\n            pred_noise = model(x_t, torch.tensor([t]))\n        # DDPM reverse formula\n        x0_pred = (x_t - (1-ab).sqrt() * pred_noise) / ab.sqrt()\n        x0_pred = x0_pred.clamp(-1, 1)\n        mean    = ab_prev.sqrt() * beta / (1-ab) * x0_pred + \\\n                  self.alpha[t].sqrt() * (1-ab_prev) / (1-ab) * x_t\n        sigma   = beta.sqrt() if t > 0 else 0\n        return mean + sigma * torch.randn_like(x_t)\n\n# Training loop skeleton\ndef diffusion_train_step(model, x0, schedule, optimiser, criterion):\n    t = torch.randint(0, schedule.T, (x0.size(0),))\n    x_t, noise = schedule.q_sample(x0, t)\n    pred_noise = model(x_t, t)\n    loss = criterion(pred_noise, noise)\n    optimiser.zero_grad(); loss.backward(); optimiser.step()\n    return loss.item()' },
    { type: 'text', body: '<h3>Latent Diffusion & Stable Diffusion</h3><p>Running T=1000 diffusion steps on 512�512 images is prohibitively slow. <strong>Latent Diffusion Models</strong> (Rombach et al., 2022) solve this: first, a VAE compresses the image to a compact latent space (64�64�4). Diffusion runs in this latent space � 8x smaller. The VAE decoder expands the final latent back to the full image. This is the architecture behind Stable Diffusion.</p><p><strong>Conditioning</strong>: Stable Diffusion uses text conditioning via CLIP embeddings, injected into the diffusion U-Net via cross-attention. The model is guided to generate images that match the text prompt.</p>' },
    { type: 'code', lang: 'python', src: '# Inference with Stable Diffusion via diffusers (pip install diffusers)\nfrom diffusers import StableDiffusionPipeline\nimport torch\n\npipe = StableDiffusionPipeline.from_pretrained(\n    "runwayml/stable-diffusion-v1-5",\n    torch_dtype=torch.float16  # use float16 on GPU\n).to("cuda" if torch.cuda.is_available() else "cpu")\n\nimage = pipe(\n    prompt="A photorealistic illustration of a data scientist working "\n           "in a futuristic office, surrounded by holographic charts",\n    negative_prompt="blurry, low quality, cartoon",\n    num_inference_steps=30,   # more steps = higher quality, slower\n    guidance_scale=7.5,       # how strongly to follow the prompt\n    height=512, width=512\n).images[0]\n\nimage.save("generated.png")\nprint("Generated image saved to generated.png")' },
    { type: 'tip', title: 'Classifier-free guidance (CFG)', body: 'Guidance scale (cfg_scale / guidance_scale) controls how strongly the image follows the prompt. Scale=1 is unconditional (ignores prompt); scale=7-8 is standard; scale>15 produces oversaturated, distorted images. During training, randomly drop the condition (10%) � the model learns both conditioned and unconditioned generation, enabling CFG at inference without a separate classifier.' }
  ]
};

L['dl-w5-l4'] = {
  duration_mins: 45,
  sections: [
    { type: 'text', body: '<h2>Self-Supervised Learning & Contrastive Methods</h2><p>Labels are expensive. Self-supervised learning (SSL) extracts supervision from the data itself � no human annotation required. Modern SSL methods have produced representations that match or exceed supervised baselines on many benchmarks, using only unlabelled data for pretraining.</p>' },
    { type: 'text', body: '<h3>Contrastive Learning � SimCLR & MoCo</h3><p>The core idea: views of the same image (different crops, flips, colour jitter) should have similar representations; views from different images should be dissimilar. The model learns by maximising agreement between positive pairs (same image, different augmentation) vs. negative pairs (different images).</p><p><strong>SimCLR</strong>: take two augmented views of each image in a batch; maximise cosine similarity between positive pairs, minimise against all negatives in the batch (NT-Xent loss). Large batch sizes are critical (4096+).</p><p><strong>MoCo</strong>: maintains a momentum encoder and a memory queue of negatives � decouples batch size from negative count. Works with much smaller batches (256).</p>' },
    { type: 'code', lang: 'python', src: 'import torch\nimport torch.nn as nn\nimport torch.nn.functional as F\nfrom torchvision import transforms\n\n# SimCLR augmentation pipeline\ndef get_simclr_transform(img_size=32):\n    return transforms.Compose([\n        transforms.RandomResizedCrop(img_size, scale=(0.2, 1.0)),\n        transforms.RandomHorizontalFlip(p=0.5),\n        transforms.RandomApply([\n            transforms.ColorJitter(brightness=0.8, contrast=0.8,\n                                   saturation=0.8, hue=0.2)\n        ], p=0.8),\n        transforms.RandomGrayscale(p=0.2),\n        transforms.GaussianBlur(kernel_size=img_size//20*2+1),\n        transforms.ToTensor(),\n        transforms.Normalize((0.4914,0.4822,0.4465), (0.2470,0.2435,0.2616))\n    ])\n\n# NT-Xent contrastive loss\ndef nt_xent_loss(z1, z2, temperature=0.5):\n    """z1, z2: (B, D) L2-normalised embeddings"""\n    B = z1.size(0)\n    z1 = F.normalize(z1, dim=1)\n    z2 = F.normalize(z2, dim=1)\n    z  = torch.cat([z1, z2], dim=0)   # (2B, D)\n    sim = z @ z.T / temperature        # (2B, 2B) cosine similarities\n    # Mask diagonal (self-similarity)\n    mask = torch.eye(2*B, dtype=bool, device=z.device)\n    sim.masked_fill_(mask, -1e9)\n    # Positive pairs: i with i+B, i+B with i\n    pos = torch.arange(B, device=z.device)\n    targets = torch.cat([pos + B, pos])  # (2B,)\n    return F.cross_entropy(sim, targets)\n\n# SimCLR encoder + projection head\nclass SimCLR(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, encoder, d_model=512, proj_dim=128):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.encoder = encoder\n        self.projector = nn.Sequential(\n            nn.Linear(d_model, d_model), nn.ReLU(),\n            nn.Linear(d_model, proj_dim)\n        )\n    def forward(self, x):\n        h = self.encoder(x)    # feature representation\n        z = self.projector(h)  # projection (used for contrastive loss)\n        return h, z' },
    { type: 'text', body: '<h3>DINO & DINO v2 � Self-Distillation</h3><p>DINO (Caron et al., 2021) uses a student-teacher framework without explicit negative pairs. The teacher is an exponential moving average (EMA) of the student. Both process different crops of the same image; the student learns to match the teacher\'s output distribution. DINO features trained on ViT models produce remarkable emergent properties � attention maps that look like semantic segmentation masks, with no segmentation labels ever seen.</p><p>DINOv2 (2023) scales this up with curated web data and is the strongest general-purpose visual feature extractor available, outperforming supervised models on many benchmarks.</p>' },
    { type: 'tip', title: 'When to use SSL pretraining', body: 'If your labelled dataset has fewer than 10K images but you have access to large unlabelled data in the same domain (e.g., unlabelled medical scans), SSL pretraining can dramatically outperform supervised training from scratch. Use DINOv2 or ImageNet-pretrained features as baselines first � SSL pretraining is only worth the compute if domain shift from ImageNet is large.' }
  ]
};

L['dl-w5-l5'] = {
  duration_mins: 50,
  sections: [
    { type: 'text', body: '<h2>Multimodal Models � CLIP, Flamingo & GPT-4V</h2><p>Multimodal models process and relate information from multiple modalities � primarily images and text, but also audio, video, and code. They power image search, visual question answering, medical image report generation, and the vision capabilities of GPT-4 and Gemini.</p>' },
    { type: 'text', body: '<h3>CLIP � Contrastive Language-Image Pretraining</h3><p>CLIP (Radford et al., OpenAI, 2021) trains an image encoder and a text encoder jointly on 400 million image-text pairs scraped from the web. Training objective: given a batch of N image-text pairs, maximise cosine similarity of matching pairs and minimise non-matching pairs (contrastive). Result: both encoders produce aligned embeddings � "a photo of a cat" and a cat image have similar representations.</p><p>Emergent capability: <strong>zero-shot classification</strong>. For any class, create a text prompt "a photo of a {class}". CLIP classifies the image by finding the text with the highest cosine similarity � without any task-specific fine-tuning.</p>' },
    { type: 'code', lang: 'python', src: '# CLIP zero-shot image classification\nimport torch\nfrom PIL import Image\nimport requests\nfrom transformers import CLIPProcessor, CLIPModel\n\nmodel     = CLIPModel.from_pretrained("openai/clip-vit-base-patch32")\nprocessor = CLIPProcessor.from_pretrained("openai/clip-vit-base-patch32")\n\n# Download a sample image\nimage = Image.open(requests.get(\n    "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/43/Cute_dog.jpg",\n    stream=True).raw)\n\n# Zero-shot classification\ncandidates = ["a photo of a dog", "a photo of a cat",\n              "a photo of a bird", "a photo of a car"]\n\ninputs  = processor(text=candidates, images=image, return_tensors="pt", padding=True)\noutputs = model(**inputs)\n\nlogits_per_image = outputs.logits_per_image  # (1, num_candidates)\nprobs = logits_per_image.softmax(dim=1)[0]\n\nfor candidate, prob in zip(candidates, probs):\n    print(f"{candidate:35s}: {prob:.3f}")\n\n# CLIP image embeddings for similarity search\nimages = [Image.open(...) for ... in image_paths]  # your image collection\ninputs = processor(images=images, return_tensors="pt")\nimage_embeds = model.get_image_features(**inputs)\nimage_embeds = image_embeds / image_embeds.norm(dim=-1, keepdim=True)  # L2 normalise\n\n# Query by text\ntext_input  = processor(text=["a sunset over mountains"], return_tensors="pt")\ntext_embed  = model.get_text_features(**text_input)\ntext_embed  = text_embed / text_embed.norm(dim=-1, keepdim=True)\nsimilarities = (image_embeds @ text_embed.T).squeeze()\nprint("Most similar image index:", similarities.argmax().item())' },
    { type: 'text', body: '<h3>Visual Language Models � Flamingo & LLaVA</h3><p><strong>Flamingo</strong> (Alayrac et al., DeepMind, 2022): interleaves vision and language � a frozen vision encoder (NFNet), cross-attention layers connecting vision to a frozen language model (Chinchilla), and a small set of learnable parameters. Achieves few-shot VQA by conditioning on image-text examples.</p><p><strong>LLaVA</strong> (Liu et al., 2023): simpler architecture � CLIP vision encoder + simple linear projection + LLaMA. Instruction-tuned on GPT-4 generated image-description pairs. Surprisingly capable with far fewer parameters.</p><p><strong>GPT-4V / Gemini</strong>: closed-source multimodal models with strong visual reasoning capabilities � can read text in images, solve geometry problems, describe medical scans, write code from wireframes.</p>' },
    { type: 'code', lang: 'python', src: '# LLaVA inference with transformers\nfrom transformers import LlavaNextProcessor, LlavaNextForConditionalGeneration\nimport torch\nfrom PIL import Image\nimport requests\n\nprocessor = LlavaNextProcessor.from_pretrained("llava-hf/llava-v1.6-mistral-7b-hf")\nmodel     = LlavaNextForConditionalGeneration.from_pretrained(\n    "llava-hf/llava-v1.6-mistral-7b-hf",\n    torch_dtype=torch.float16, device_map="auto"\n)\n\nimage = Image.open(requests.get("https://example.com/chart.png", stream=True).raw)\n\nconversation = [{\n    "role": "user",\n    "content": [\n        {"type": "image"},\n        {"type": "text", "text": "Describe what this chart shows and what insights you can extract."}\n    ]\n}]\n\nprompt = processor.apply_chat_template(conversation, add_generation_prompt=True)\ninputs = processor(prompt, image, return_tensors="pt").to(model.device)\n\nout = model.generate(**inputs, max_new_tokens=300, do_sample=False)\nprint(processor.decode(out[0], skip_special_tokens=True))' },
    { type: 'tip', title: 'CLIP for image search at scale', body: 'CLIP embeddings + approximate nearest-neighbour search (FAISS, Pinecone, pgvector) enables semantic image search at web scale. Index millions of image embeddings offline; at query time, embed the search text and find the closest image vectors. No image labels needed � CLIP understands open-vocabulary queries.' }
  ]
};

