L['dl-w6-l1'] = {
  duration_mins: 50,
  sections: [
    { type: 'text', body: '<h2>Working with Imbalanced Datasets in Deep Learning</h2><p>Real-world classification datasets are rarely balanced. Medical diagnosis (1% cancer rate), fraud detection (0.1% fraud), rare event prediction � severe class imbalance causes networks to learn the majority class and predict it for everything, achieving 99% accuracy by ignoring the class we care most about.</p>' },
    { type: 'text', body: '<h3>Techniques for Handling Imbalance</h3><ul><li><strong>Weighted cross-entropy</strong>: assign higher loss weight to minority classes. Weight = N_total / (N_classes � N_class). Simple, effective, zero overhead.</li><li><strong>Oversampling (SMOTE)</strong>: synthesise new minority examples by interpolating between existing ones. For images: use data augmentation to generate more minority views.</li><li><strong>Undersampling</strong>: reduce majority class. Often wastes data � combine with oversampling.</li><li><strong>Focal Loss</strong>: down-weight easy examples dynamically. Ideal for object detection where background dominates.</li><li><strong>Class-balanced sampling</strong>: sample with probability proportional to 1/class_frequency. PyTorch <code>WeightedRandomSampler</code>.</li></ul>' },
    { type: 'code', lang: 'python', src: 'import torch\nimport torch.nn as nn\nfrom torch.utils.data import WeightedRandomSampler, DataLoader\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Method 1: Weighted CrossEntropyLoss\ndef compute_class_weights(labels, device="cpu"):\n    classes, counts = np.unique(labels, return_counts=True)\n    weights = len(labels) / (len(classes) * counts)\n    return torch.tensor(weights, dtype=torch.float32, device=device)\n\nlabels = [0]*900 + [1]*100  # 90% class 0, 10% class 1\nclass_weights = compute_class_weights(labels)\ncriterion = nn.CrossEntropyLoss(weight=class_weights)\nprint(f"Class weights: {class_weights.tolist()}")  # [0.56, 5.0] � minority gets 9x weight\n\n# Method 2: WeightedRandomSampler (balances batches during sampling)\nsample_weights = torch.tensor([class_weights[l] for l in labels])\nsampler = WeightedRandomSampler(sample_weights, num_samples=len(labels), replacement=True)\n# loader = DataLoader(dataset, batch_size=32, sampler=sampler)  # each batch ~50/50\n\n# Method 3: Focal Loss\nclass FocalLoss(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, gamma=2.0, alpha=0.25):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.gamma = gamma\n        self.alpha = alpha\n\n    def forward(self, inputs, targets):\n        ce_loss = nn.functional.cross_entropy(inputs, targets, reduction="none")\n        pt = torch.exp(-ce_loss)  # probability of correct class\n        focal_loss = self.alpha * (1 - pt) ** self.gamma * ce_loss\n        return focal_loss.mean()' },
    { type: 'code', lang: 'python', src: '# Evaluation with imbalanced data � never use accuracy\nfrom sklearn.metrics import classification_report, roc_auc_score, average_precision_score\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef evaluate_imbalanced(model, loader, device="cpu"):\n    model.eval()\n    all_probs, all_labels = [], []\n    with torch.no_grad():\n        for x, y in loader:\n            logits = model(x.to(device))\n            probs  = torch.softmax(logits, dim=1)[:, 1]  # minority class prob\n            all_probs.extend(probs.cpu().tolist())\n            all_labels.extend(y.tolist())\n\n    y_prob = np.array(all_probs)\n    y_true = np.array(all_labels)\n\n    # Find threshold that maximises F1\n    thresholds = np.linspace(0.1, 0.9, 81)\n    f1s = []\n    from sklearn.metrics import f1_score\n    for t in thresholds:\n        f1s.append(f1_score(y_true, (y_prob >= t).astype(int)))\n    best_thresh = thresholds[np.argmax(f1s)]\n    y_pred = (y_prob >= best_thresh).astype(int)\n\n    print(f"ROC-AUC:          {roc_auc_score(y_true, y_prob):.4f}")\n    print(f"PR-AUC:           {average_precision_score(y_true, y_prob):.4f}")\n    print(f"Best threshold:   {best_thresh:.2f}")\n    print(classification_report(y_true, y_pred, target_names=[\'Majority\',\'Minority\']))\n    return best_thresh' },
    { type: 'tip', title: 'Use PR-AUC not ROC-AUC for severe imbalance', body: 'With 1% minority class, a model that flags everything gets ROC-AUC=0.5. But PR-AUC (Precision-Recall AUC) is sensitive to minority class performance because precision is defined over positive predictions only. When positive rate < 5%, always report PR-AUC as the primary metric.' }
  ]
};

L['dl-w6-l2'] = {
  duration_mins: 50,
  sections: [
    { type: 'text', body: '<h2>Model Interpretability � Grad-CAM & Attribution Methods</h2><p>Deep learning models are often criticised as "black boxes." Interpretability tools reveal <em>why</em> a model made a prediction � which input features drove the decision. In safety-critical applications (medical diagnosis, credit scoring, autonomous driving), this is not optional.</p>' },
    { type: 'text', body: '<h3>Grad-CAM � Gradient-weighted Class Activation Mapping</h3><p>Selvaraju et al. (2017): computes the gradient of the target class score with respect to the final convolutional feature maps. The gradient magnitude for each channel tells us how important that channel\'s feature map is for the classification. A weighted average of feature maps produces a heatmap highlighting the most discriminative image regions.</p>' },
    { type: 'code', lang: 'python', src: 'import torch\nimport torch.nn as nn\nimport numpy as np\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport cv2\nfrom torchvision import models, transforms\nfrom PIL import Image\n\nclass GradCAM:\n    def __init__(self, model, target_layer):\n        self.model = model\n        self.gradients = None\n        self.activations = None\n        target_layer.register_forward_hook(\n            lambda m, i, o: self.__setattr__(\'activations\', o.detach())\n        )\n        target_layer.register_full_backward_hook(\n            lambda m, gi, go: self.__setattr__(\'gradients\', go[0].detach())\n        )\n\n    def __call__(self, x, class_idx=None):\n        self.model.eval()\n        logits = self.model(x)\n        if class_idx is None:\n            class_idx = logits.argmax(dim=1).item()\n        self.model.zero_grad()\n        logits[0, class_idx].backward()\n        # Grad-CAM: weight activations by global-average-pooled gradients\n        weights = self.gradients.mean(dim=(2, 3), keepdim=True)  # (1,C,1,1)\n        cam = (weights * self.activations).sum(dim=1).squeeze()   # (H,W)\n        cam = torch.relu(cam)                                      # ReLU: only positive\n        cam = (cam - cam.min()) / (cam.max() - cam.min() + 1e-8)  # normalise 0-1\n        return cam.numpy(), class_idx\n\n# Usage with ResNet\nmodel     = models.resnet18(weights=models.ResNet18_Weights.IMAGENET1K_V1)\ngradcam   = GradCAM(model, model.layer4[-1].conv2)\n\ntransform = transforms.Compose([\n    transforms.Resize(224), transforms.CenterCrop(224),\n    transforms.ToTensor(),\n    transforms.Normalize([0.485,0.456,0.406],[0.229,0.224,0.225])\n])\nimage = Image.open("dog.jpg")  # any image\nx = transform(image).unsqueeze(0)\ncam, pred_class = gradcam(x)\n\n# Overlay heatmap on image\ncam_resized = cv2.resize(cam, (224, 224))\nheatmap = cv2.applyColorMap((cam_resized*255).astype(np.uint8), cv2.COLORMAP_JET)\nimg_np  = np.array(image.resize((224,224)))\noverlay = cv2.addWeighted(img_np, 0.6, heatmap, 0.4, 0)\nplt.figure(figsize=(10,4))\nplt.subplot(1,3,1); plt.imshow(img_np); plt.title("Original"); plt.axis("off")\nplt.subplot(1,3,2); plt.imshow(cam_resized, cmap="jet"); plt.title("Grad-CAM"); plt.axis("off")\nplt.subplot(1,3,3); plt.imshow(overlay); plt.title("Overlay"); plt.axis("off")\nplt.tight_layout(); plt.show()' },
    { type: 'text', body: '<h3>SHAP & Integrated Gradients</h3><p><strong>Integrated Gradients</strong> (Sundararajan et al., 2017): attributes prediction to each input feature by integrating gradients along the path from a baseline (black image) to the actual input. Provably satisfies axioms of completeness and sensitivity. Available in the Captum library.</p><p><strong>SHAP</strong>: game-theoretic feature attribution (Shapley values). Consistent across model classes. For deep learning, DeepSHAP combines SHAP with backpropagation for efficiency.</p>' },
    { type: 'code', lang: 'python', src: '# Captum: unified interpretability for PyTorch\n# pip install captum\nfrom captum.attr import IntegratedGradients, GradientShap\n\nig = IntegratedGradients(model)\nbaseline = torch.zeros_like(x)  # black image as baseline\nattributions, delta = ig.attribute(\n    x, baseline, target=pred_class, return_convergence_delta=True, n_steps=50\n)\nprint(f"Attribution shape: {attributions.shape}")  # (1,3,224,224)\nprint(f"Convergence delta: {delta.item():.6f}")    # should be near 0\n\n# Visualise attribution heatmap\nattr = attributions[0].abs().sum(0).numpy()  # sum over channels\nplt.imshow(attr, cmap="hot"); plt.colorbar()\nplt.title("Integrated Gradients attribution map"); plt.axis("off"); plt.show()' },
    { type: 'tip', title: 'Interpretability != Explainability', body: 'Grad-CAM shows WHERE the model looked; it does not explain WHY it made the decision (the model may attend to the right region for the wrong reasons). Always sanity-check by testing whether removing the highlighted region changes the prediction. For regulatory compliance (GDPR, medical AI), feature attribution alone may be insufficient � you may need a fundamentally interpretable model (logistic regression, decision tree) on structured features.' }
  ]
};

L['dl-w6-l3'] = {
  duration_mins: 50,
  sections: [
    { type: 'text', body: '<h2>Model Compression � Pruning, Quantisation & Knowledge Distillation</h2><p>A ResNet-50 with 25 million parameters runs well on a server GPU but cannot run on a mobile phone. Model compression techniques reduce size and latency while preserving most of the accuracy � enabling deployment on edge devices, reducing serving costs, and decreasing inference latency.</p>' },
    { type: 'text', body: '<h3>Quantisation</h3><p>Replace 32-bit floats (FP32) with lower-precision representations. INT8 (8-bit integers) uses 4x less memory and runs 2-4x faster on CPUs and GPUs with INT8 tensor cores. Accuracy loss is typically less than 1% with post-training quantisation.</p><ul><li><strong>Dynamic quantisation</strong>: weights quantised statically, activations quantised dynamically at runtime. Zero calibration data needed. Best for RNNs and transformers.</li><li><strong>Static quantisation</strong>: both weights and activations quantised using calibration data. Faster inference, requires a small calibration set.</li><li><strong>Quantisation-aware training (QAT)</strong>: simulate quantisation during training with "fake quantisation" nodes. Best accuracy.</li></ul>' },
    { type: 'code', lang: 'python', src: 'import torch\nimport torch.nn as nn\n\n# Dynamic quantisation (simplest � good for LSTM, linear layers)\nmodel = SentimentLSTM(vocab_size=10000)  # your model\nquantised = torch.quantization.quantize_dynamic(\n    model, {nn.Linear, nn.LSTM}, dtype=torch.qint8\n)\nprint(f"Original size: {sum(p.numel() for p in model.parameters())*4/1e6:.1f} MB")\nprint("Quantised model created (weights in INT8)")\n\n# Static quantisation for CNNs\ndef quantise_static(model, calibration_loader):\n    model.eval()\n    # Fuse BN into Conv before quantising\n    model_fused = torch.quantization.fuse_modules(model, [[\'conv\',\'bn\',\'relu\']])\n    model_fused.qconfig = torch.quantization.get_default_qconfig(\'fbgemm\')\n    model_prep = torch.quantization.prepare(model_fused)\n    # Calibration � run representative data through\n    with torch.no_grad():\n        for x, _ in calibration_loader:\n            model_prep(x)\n    model_int8 = torch.quantization.convert(model_prep)\n    return model_int8\n\n# Benchmark inference speed\nimport time\n\ndef benchmark(model, x, n=100):\n    model.eval()\n    with torch.no_grad():\n        # Warmup\n        for _ in range(10): model(x)\n        t0 = time.perf_counter()\n        for _ in range(n): model(x)\n        return (time.perf_counter() - t0) / n * 1000  # ms\n\n# print(f"FP32: {benchmark(model, x):.2f} ms")\n# print(f"INT8: {benchmark(quantised, x):.2f} ms")' },
    { type: 'text', body: '<h3>Knowledge Distillation</h3><p>Train a small <strong>student</strong> model to mimic a large <strong>teacher</strong> model. The student learns from soft teacher outputs (class probability distributions) rather than hard labels � soft outputs contain more information ("this looks 70% like a cat, 20% like a fox") and serve as richer supervision. Temperature T softens the distribution: softmax(logits/T).</p>' },
    { type: 'code', lang: 'python', src: 'import torch\nimport torch.nn as nn\nimport torch.nn.functional as F\n\ndef distillation_loss(student_logits, teacher_logits, labels,\n                      T=4.0, alpha=0.7):\n    """\n    student_logits: raw student outputs\n    teacher_logits: raw teacher outputs (detached)\n    labels: ground truth\n    T: temperature (higher = softer distributions)\n    alpha: weight for distillation loss (1-alpha for CE loss)\n    """\n    # Soft targets from teacher\n    soft_teacher = F.softmax(teacher_logits / T, dim=1)\n    soft_student = F.log_softmax(student_logits / T, dim=1)\n    distill = F.kl_div(soft_student, soft_teacher, reduction=\'batchmean\') * T**2\n\n    # Hard targets (standard CE)\n    hard = F.cross_entropy(student_logits, labels)\n\n    return alpha * distill + (1 - alpha) * hard\n\n# Training with distillation\n# teacher.eval()  # teacher in eval mode\n# for x, y in loader:\n#     with torch.no_grad():\n#         teacher_logits = teacher(x)\n#     student_logits = student(x)\n#     loss = distillation_loss(student_logits, teacher_logits.detach(), y)\n#     loss.backward(); optimiser.step(); optimiser.zero_grad()' },
    { type: 'tip', title: 'ONNX for cross-framework deployment', body: 'Export any PyTorch model to ONNX (Open Neural Network Exchange) for framework-agnostic deployment: `torch.onnx.export(model, dummy_input, "model.onnx", opset_version=17)`. Run with ONNX Runtime for 1.5-3x speedup over PyTorch CPU inference. ONNX Runtime is the recommended engine for production serving on CPUs.' }
  ]
};

L['dl-w6-l4'] = {
  duration_mins: 45,
  sections: [
    { type: 'text', body: '<h2>Hyperparameter Tuning for Deep Learning</h2><p>Deep learning has many more hyperparameters than classical ML: learning rate, schedule, batch size, dropout rates, weight decay, architecture depth and width, activation functions, normalisation strategy. Systematic search is essential.</p>' },
    { type: 'text', body: '<h3>What to Tune First (Importance Ordering)</h3><ol><li><strong>Learning rate</strong>: single highest-impact parameter. Run LR finder or try {1e-4, 3e-4, 1e-3, 3e-3}.</li><li><strong>Batch size</strong>: larger batches need larger LR (linear scaling rule). GPU memory constrained.</li><li><strong>Regularisation</strong>: weight decay {1e-5, 1e-4, 1e-3}; dropout {0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.5}.</li><li><strong>Architecture</strong>: depth and width � tune last (expensive, and pre-trained models often fix this).</li><li><strong>Data augmentation</strong>: often as impactful as architecture changes.</li></ol>' },
    { type: 'code', lang: 'python', src: '# Optuna: state-of-art Bayesian hyperparameter optimisation\n# pip install optuna\nimport optuna\nimport torch\nimport torch.nn as nn\n\ndef objective(trial):\n    # Define hyperparameter search space\n    lr           = trial.suggest_float("lr", 1e-5, 1e-2, log=True)\n    weight_decay = trial.suggest_float("weight_decay", 1e-6, 1e-2, log=True)\n    dropout      = trial.suggest_float("dropout", 0.1, 0.5)\n    hidden_dim   = trial.suggest_categorical("hidden_dim", [128, 256, 512])\n    n_layers     = trial.suggest_int("n_layers", 1, 4)\n\n    # Build and train model\n    model = build_model(hidden_dim, n_layers, dropout)\n    optimiser = torch.optim.AdamW(model.parameters(), lr=lr, weight_decay=weight_decay)\n    val_loss = train_and_evaluate(model, optimiser, n_epochs=10)\n\n    return val_loss  # Optuna minimises this\n\n# Run optimisation (parallelisable across GPUs)\nstudy = optuna.create_study(direction="minimize",\n                             sampler=optuna.samplers.TPESampler(seed=42),\n                             pruner=optuna.pruners.MedianPruner())\nstudy.optimize(objective, n_trials=50, timeout=3600)  # 50 trials or 1 hour\n\nprint(f"Best params:    {study.best_params}")\nprint(f"Best val_loss:  {study.best_value:.6f}")\n\n# Optuna visualisation\nimport optuna.visualization as viz\nfig = viz.plot_param_importances(study)\nfig.show()' },
    { type: 'code', lang: 'python', src: '# LR Finder � find the best learning rate in one epoch\ndef lr_finder(model, train_loader, start_lr=1e-7, end_lr=10, n_iters=100):\n    model.train()\n    optimiser = torch.optim.AdamW(model.parameters(), lr=start_lr)\n    scheduler = torch.optim.lr_scheduler.ExponentialLR(\n        optimiser, gamma=(end_lr/start_lr)**(1/n_iters)\n    )\n    best_loss = None; losses = []; lrs = []\n\n    for i, (x, y) in enumerate(train_loader):\n        if i >= n_iters: break\n        optimiser.zero_grad()\n        loss = nn.CrossEntropyLoss()(model(x), y)\n        loss.backward(); optimiser.step(); scheduler.step()\n        current_lr = optimiser.param_groups[0]["lr"]\n        losses.append(loss.item()); lrs.append(current_lr)\n        if best_loss is None or loss.item() < best_loss:\n            best_loss = loss.item()\n        if loss.item() > 4 * best_loss:  # loss exploding\n            break\n\n    import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n    plt.figure(figsize=(8,4))\n    plt.plot(lrs, losses); plt.xscale("log")\n    plt.xlabel("Learning Rate"); plt.ylabel("Loss")\n    plt.title("LR Finder � choose LR just before loss rises")\n    plt.show()\n    return lrs, losses' },
    { type: 'tip', title: 'Warmup is essential for large LR', body: 'Linearly increase LR from 0 to max_lr over the first 5-10% of training steps before decaying. Without warmup, a large initial LR can corrupt randomly-initialised or pretrained weights in the first few batches. OneCycleLR handles this automatically.' }
  ]
};

L['dl-w6-l5'] = {
  duration_mins: 55,
  sections: [
    { type: 'text', body: '<h2>Deploying Deep Learning Models</h2><p>A trained model that lives only in a Jupyter notebook helps nobody. Deployment means making the model accessible to real users through reliable, scalable infrastructure. This lesson covers the full deployment stack: exporting, serving, and monitoring deep learning models.</p>' },
    { type: 'text', body: '<h3>Export Formats</h3><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.9rem"><thead><tr><th style="border:1px solid #444;padding:6px">Format</th><th style="border:1px solid #444;padding:6px">Use case</th><th style="border:1px solid #444;padding:6px">Runtime</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="border:1px solid #444;padding:6px">TorchScript</td><td style="border:1px solid #444;padding:6px">C++ serving, mobile</td><td style="border:1px solid #444;padding:6px">LibTorch</td></tr><tr><td style="border:1px solid #444;padding:6px">ONNX</td><td style="border:1px solid #444;padding:6px">Cross-framework, CPU optimised</td><td style="border:1px solid #444;padding:6px">ONNX Runtime</td></tr><tr><td style="border:1px solid #444;padding:6px">TensorRT</td><td style="border:1px solid #444;padding:6px">GPU inference, maximum speed</td><td style="border:1px solid #444;padding:6px">NVIDIA TRT</td></tr><tr><td style="border:1px solid #444;padding:6px">CoreML</td><td style="border:1px solid #444;padding:6px">Apple devices (iPhone, Mac)</td><td style="border:1px solid #444;padding:6px">CoreML framework</td></tr><tr><td style="border:1px solid #444;padding:6px">TFLite</td><td style="border:1px solid #444;padding:6px">Android, embedded</td><td style="border:1px solid #444;padding:6px">TFLite runtime</td></tr></tbody></table>' },
    { type: 'code', lang: 'python', src: 'import torch\nimport onnx\nimport onnxruntime as ort\nimport numpy as np\n\n# 1. Export to ONNX\nmodel.eval()\ndummy = torch.randn(1, 3, 224, 224)  # match your input shape\ntorch.onnx.export(\n    model, dummy, "model.onnx",\n    opset_version=17,\n    input_names=["input"],\n    output_names=["output"],\n    dynamic_axes={"input": {0: "batch_size"}, "output": {0: "batch_size"}}\n)\nprint("ONNX export successful")\n\n# 2. Validate ONNX graph\nonnx_model = onnx.load("model.onnx")\nonnx.checker.check_model(onnx_model)\nprint("ONNX model graph is valid")\n\n# 3. Run inference with ONNX Runtime\nsess = ort.InferenceSession("model.onnx",\n    providers=["CUDAExecutionProvider", "CPUExecutionProvider"])\ninput_name = sess.get_inputs()[0].name\n\nx_np = np.random.randn(1, 3, 224, 224).astype(np.float32)\noutputs = sess.run(None, {input_name: x_np})\nprint(f"ORT output shape: {outputs[0].shape}")\n\n# 4. Compare PyTorch vs ONNX Runtime outputs\nwith torch.no_grad():\n    pt_out = model(torch.tensor(x_np)).numpy()\nort_out = outputs[0]\nmax_diff = np.abs(pt_out - ort_out).max()\nprint(f"Max numerical difference (PyTorch vs ORT): {max_diff:.2e}")  # should be < 1e-5' },
    { type: 'code', lang: 'python', src: '# FastAPI serving endpoint for ONNX model\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, File, UploadFile\nfrom PIL import Image\nimport io, numpy as np\nimport onnxruntime as ort\nfrom torchvision import transforms\n\napp = FastAPI(title="Image Classifier API")\nsess = ort.InferenceSession("model.onnx",\n    providers=["CUDAExecutionProvider","CPUExecutionProvider"])\n\nINPUT_NAME = sess.get_inputs()[0].name\nCLASS_NAMES = ["cat","dog","bird","fish","horse","other"]  # your classes\n\npreprocess = transforms.Compose([\n    transforms.Resize(256), transforms.CenterCrop(224),\n    transforms.ToTensor(),\n    transforms.Normalize([0.485,0.456,0.406],[0.229,0.224,0.225])\n])\n\n@app.post("/classify")\nasync def classify(file: UploadFile = File(...)):\n    img = Image.open(io.BytesIO(await file.read())).convert("RGB")\n    x   = preprocess(img).unsqueeze(0).numpy()\n    out = sess.run(None, {INPUT_NAME: x})[0]\n    probs = np.exp(out) / np.exp(out).sum()  # softmax\n    top5  = probs[0].argsort()[::-1][:5]\n    return {"predictions": [\n        {"class": CLASS_NAMES[i], "probability": round(float(probs[0][i]), 4)}\n        for i in top5\n    ]}\n\n@app.get("/health")\ndef health(): return {"status": "ok"}' },
    { type: 'tip', title: 'Triton Inference Server for production scale', body: 'NVIDIA Triton Inference Server handles concurrent requests, dynamic batching, model pipelines (ensemble of models), and multi-GPU scheduling automatically. For high-throughput production serving (>100 QPS), Triton is the industry standard. It supports TorchScript, ONNX, TensorRT, and TFLite natively.' }
  ]
};

