L['dl-w7-l1'] = {
  duration_mins: 50,
  sections: [
    { type: 'text', body: '<h2>Reinforcement Learning with Deep Networks</h2><p>Reinforcement Learning (RL) trains agents to maximise cumulative reward through trial and error. Deep RL replaces hand-crafted features with neural network function approximators � enabling agents to learn directly from raw pixels. Deep RL mastered Go (AlphaGo), trained GPT models to follow instructions (RLHF), and powers robotics and game-playing AI.</p>' },
    { type: 'text', body: '<h3>The RL Framework</h3><p>An <strong>agent</strong> interacts with an <strong>environment</strong> in discrete timesteps: observe state s, take action a, receive reward r, transition to new state s\'. The goal: learn a <strong>policy</strong> p(a|s) that maximises expected cumulative discounted reward: G = S ?? r? (?=0.99 discounts future rewards).</p><p><strong>Value function</strong> V(s): expected return from state s. <strong>Action-value function</strong> Q(s,a): expected return from state s taking action a, then following policy p.</p>' },
    { type: 'code', lang: 'python', src: '# Deep Q-Network (DQN) � plays Atari from pixels\nimport torch\nimport torch.nn as nn\nimport torch.nn.functional as F\nimport random\nfrom collections import deque\n\nclass DQN(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, n_actions):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.conv = nn.Sequential(\n            nn.Conv2d(4, 32, 8, 4),  nn.ReLU(),  # 4 stacked frames\n            nn.Conv2d(32, 64, 4, 2), nn.ReLU(),\n            nn.Conv2d(64, 64, 3, 1), nn.ReLU()\n        )\n        self.fc = nn.Sequential(\n            nn.Flatten(),\n            nn.Linear(64*7*7, 512), nn.ReLU(),\n            nn.Linear(512, n_actions)\n        )\n    def forward(self, x): return self.fc(self.conv(x / 255.0))\n\n# Replay buffer: sample random transitions for stable training\nclass ReplayBuffer:\n    def __init__(self, capacity=100_000):\n        self.buffer = deque(maxlen=capacity)\n    def push(self, s, a, r, s_next, done):\n        self.buffer.append((s, a, r, s_next, done))\n    def sample(self, batch_size):\n        batch = random.sample(self.buffer, batch_size)\n        s, a, r, s2, d = zip(*batch)\n        return (torch.tensor(s, dtype=torch.float32),\n                torch.tensor(a), torch.tensor(r, dtype=torch.float32),\n                torch.tensor(s2, dtype=torch.float32),\n                torch.tensor(d, dtype=torch.float32))\n    def __len__(self): return len(self.buffer)\n\ndef dqn_update(online, target, buffer, optimiser, gamma=0.99, batch_size=32):\n    s, a, r, s2, done = buffer.sample(batch_size)\n    q_vals  = online(s).gather(1, a.unsqueeze(1)).squeeze()\n    with torch.no_grad():\n        # Double DQN: online selects action, target evaluates Q-value\n        next_a  = online(s2).argmax(1)\n        q_next  = target(s2).gather(1, next_a.unsqueeze(1)).squeeze()\n        targets = r + gamma * q_next * (1 - done)\n    loss = F.huber_loss(q_vals, targets)\n    optimiser.zero_grad(); loss.backward(); optimiser.step()\n    return loss.item()' },
    { type: 'text', body: '<h3>Policy Gradient � REINFORCE & PPO</h3><p>Value-based methods (DQN) learn Q-values and derive a policy. <strong>Policy gradient</strong> methods learn the policy directly. REINFORCE: update policy parameters in proportion to the reward received, weighted by the log-probability of the action. PPO (Proximal Policy Optimisation, Schulman et al. 2017): constrains policy updates to a trust region � the default RL algorithm for instruction-tuning LLMs (RLHF).</p>' },
    { type: 'tip', title: 'Stable Baselines 3 for practical RL', body: 'Implementing RL from scratch is error-prone. Use Stable Baselines 3 (SB3) � well-tested, PyTorch-based implementations of DQN, PPO, SAC, and A2C. `model = PPO("MlpPolicy", env, verbose=1); model.learn(1_000_000)` is a complete training loop. Focus on environment design, reward shaping, and evaluation � not RL algorithm implementation.' }
  ]
};

L['dl-w7-l2'] = {
  duration_mins: 50,
  sections: [
    { type: 'text', body: '<h2>Graph Neural Networks</h2><p>Standard deep learning assumes inputs are grids (images) or sequences (text). But many real-world data structures are <strong>graphs</strong>: molecules (atoms = nodes, bonds = edges), social networks, knowledge bases, road networks, protein interaction networks. Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) extend deep learning to arbitrary graph structures.</p>' },
    { type: 'text', body: '<h3>Message Passing � The Core Operation</h3><p>At each layer, each node aggregates messages from its neighbours, then updates its own representation. This is called the <strong>message passing</strong> framework:</p><p style="text-align:center;padding:.5rem;background:var(--fog);border-radius:6px;">h_v^(k) = UPDATE(h_v^(k-1), AGGREGATE({h_u^(k-1) : u ? N(v)}))</p><p>After K layers, each node\'s representation contains information from its K-hop neighbourhood. Three popular architectures:</p><ul><li><strong>GCN</strong>: average neighbours (mean aggregation)</li><li><strong>GraphSAGE</strong>: sample neighbours, concat with self, linear + ReLU</li><li><strong>GAT</strong>: attention-weighted aggregation � different neighbours contribute differently</li></ul>' },
    { type: 'code', lang: 'python', src: '# PyTorch Geometric (PyG) � pip install torch-geometric\nimport torch\nimport torch.nn as nn\nimport torch.nn.functional as F\nfrom torch_geometric.nn import GCNConv, GATConv, global_mean_pool\nfrom torch_geometric.data import Data, DataLoader\n\n# Node classification with GCN\nclass GCN(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, in_feats, hidden, num_classes, dropout=0.5):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.conv1 = GCNConv(in_feats, hidden)\n        self.conv2 = GCNConv(hidden, num_classes)\n        self.dropout = dropout\n\n    def forward(self, x, edge_index):\n        x = F.dropout(x, p=self.dropout, training=self.training)\n        x = F.relu(self.conv1(x, edge_index))\n        x = F.dropout(x, p=self.dropout, training=self.training)\n        return self.conv2(x, edge_index)   # raw logits\n\n# Graph classification with Graph Attention Network + global pooling\nclass GraphGAT(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, in_feats, hidden, num_classes, heads=4):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.gat1 = GATConv(in_feats, hidden, heads=heads, concat=True)\n        self.gat2 = GATConv(hidden*heads, hidden, heads=1, concat=False)\n        self.classifier = nn.Linear(hidden, num_classes)\n\n    def forward(self, x, edge_index, batch):\n        x = F.elu(self.gat1(x, edge_index))\n        x = F.elu(self.gat2(x, edge_index))\n        x = global_mean_pool(x, batch)  # aggregate node features to graph level\n        return self.classifier(x)\n\n# Create a simple graph\nedge_index = torch.tensor([[0,1,1,2],[1,0,2,1]], dtype=torch.long)  # bidirectional\nnode_features = torch.randn(3, 16)  # 3 nodes, 16 features each\ngraph = Data(x=node_features, edge_index=edge_index)\nprint(graph)' },
    { type: 'text', body: '<h3>GNN Applications</h3><ul><li><strong>Drug discovery</strong>: predict molecular properties (solubility, toxicity, binding affinity) from molecular graphs</li><li><strong>Recommendation systems</strong>: user-item interaction graph; node embeddings for collaborative filtering</li><li><strong>Knowledge graphs</strong>: entity/relation embeddings for link prediction (Google Knowledge Graph)</li><li><strong>Traffic prediction</strong>: road network as graph; spatiotemporal GNNs for ETAs (Google Maps)</li><li><strong>AlphaFold 2</strong>: protein structure prediction uses a graph of amino acid residues</li></ul>' },
    { type: 'tip', title: 'Over-smoothing in deep GNNs', body: 'Adding too many GNN layers causes over-smoothing � all node representations converge to the same value (like running heat diffusion to equilibrium). Most effective GNNs use only 2-4 layers. For tasks requiring long-range interactions, add skip connections, use jumping knowledge networks, or switch to a graph transformer.' }
  ]
};

L['dl-w7-l3'] = {
  duration_mins: 50,
  sections: [
    { type: 'text', body: '<h2>Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning � LoRA & Adapters</h2><p>Full fine-tuning of large models (7B+ parameters) requires massive compute and produces a separate copy of the full model for each task. Parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) methods achieve competitive performance by training only a tiny fraction of parameters � making LLM customisation accessible on consumer hardware.</p>' },
    { type: 'text', body: '<h3>LoRA � Low-Rank Adaptation</h3><p>Hu et al. (2021): hypothesise that the weight update matrix ?W during fine-tuning has low intrinsic rank. Instead of updating W directly, freeze W and inject a low-rank decomposition: ?W = B�A where A ? R^(r�d) and B ? R^(d�r), r << d (typically r=4, 8, or 16). Total trainable parameters: 2�d�r vs d� for full fine-tuning � a 1000x reduction for r=8, d=4096. At inference, merge: W_new = W + a?W (a=lora_alpha/r scales the adaptation).</p>' },
    { type: 'code', lang: 'python', src: '# LoRA implementation from scratch\nimport torch\nimport torch.nn as nn\nimport math\n\nclass LoRALinear(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, in_features, out_features, r=8, lora_alpha=16, dropout=0.1):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.r = r\n        self.scale = lora_alpha / r\n        self.linear = nn.Linear(in_features, out_features)  # pretrained, frozen\n        self.lora_A = nn.Parameter(torch.randn(r, in_features) / math.sqrt(in_features))\n        self.lora_B = nn.Parameter(torch.zeros(out_features, r))  # zero init\n        self.dropout = nn.Dropout(dropout)\n        self.linear.weight.requires_grad = False  # freeze original weight\n\n    def forward(self, x):\n        base  = self.linear(x)\n        lora  = self.dropout(x) @ self.lora_A.T @ self.lora_B.T\n        return base + self.scale * lora\n\n    def merge(self):\n        """Merge LoRA into base weight for faster inference."""\n        with torch.no_grad():\n            self.linear.weight.data += self.scale * (self.lora_B @ self.lora_A)\n        self.linear.weight.requires_grad = False\n\n# Apply LoRA to a transformer model\ndef apply_lora(model, r=8, lora_alpha=16, target_modules=(\"q_proj\",\"v_proj\")):\n    """Replace target linear layers with LoRA versions."""\n    for name, module in model.named_modules():\n        if isinstance(module, nn.Linear) and any(t in name for t in target_modules):\n            parent = dict(model.named_modules())[name.rsplit(\'.\',1)[0]]\n            lora = LoRALinear(module.in_features, module.out_features, r, lora_alpha)\n            lora.linear.weight.data = module.weight.data.clone()\n            setattr(parent, name.split(\'.\')[-1], lora)\n    trainable = sum(p.numel() for p in model.parameters() if p.requires_grad)\n    total     = sum(p.numel() for p in model.parameters())\n    print(f"Trainable: {trainable:,} / {total:,} ({100*trainable/total:.2f}%)")\n    return model' },
    { type: 'code', lang: 'python', src: '# QLoRA: 4-bit quantised base model + LoRA (fits 70B models on a single A100)\n# pip install peft bitsandbytes transformers\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, AutoTokenizer, BitsAndBytesConfig\nfrom peft import LoraConfig, get_peft_model, TaskType\n\n# 4-bit quantised loading\nquant_config = BitsAndBytesConfig(\n    load_in_4bit=True,\n    bnb_4bit_compute_dtype=torch.float16,\n    bnb_4bit_use_double_quant=True,\n    bnb_4bit_quant_type="nf4"  # NF4: optimal for normally-distributed weights\n)\nbase_model = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\n    "meta-llama/Llama-2-7b-hf",\n    quantization_config=quant_config, device_map="auto"\n)\n\n# Add LoRA adapters\nlora_config = LoraConfig(\n    r=16, lora_alpha=32, target_modules=["q_proj","k_proj","v_proj","o_proj"],\n    lora_dropout=0.05, bias="none", task_type=TaskType.CAUSAL_LM\n)\nmodel = get_peft_model(base_model, lora_config)\nmodel.print_trainable_parameters()  # ~0.1% of total params' },
    { type: 'tip', title: 'r=8 is a good starting LoRA rank', body: 'Rank controls the expressiveness vs. parameter count tradeoff. r=4 for style/tone adaptation; r=8 for task fine-tuning (most common); r=16-64 for domain adaptation requiring large shifts from the pretrained distribution. Higher rank with lower lora_alpha dampens the adaptation strength.' }
  ]
};

L['dl-w7-l4'] = {
  duration_mins: 45,
  sections: [
    { type: 'text', body: '<h2>Ethical AI & Responsible Deep Learning</h2><p>Deep learning systems make consequential decisions: medical diagnoses, loan approvals, content moderation, facial recognition, hiring. These systems can perpetuate and amplify biases present in training data, behave unpredictably on out-of-distribution inputs, and make decisions that are difficult to audit. Responsible AI is not a soft skill � it is a technical discipline with measurable metrics and engineering solutions.</p>' },
    { type: 'text', body: '<h3>Sources of Bias in DL Systems</h3><ul><li><strong>Data bias</strong>: training data over- or under-represents certain groups. Example: facial recognition trained predominantly on lighter-skinned faces from Western sources � error rates 10-35% higher for darker-skinned women (Buolamwini & Gebru, 2018).</li><li><strong>Label bias</strong>: annotators bring their own biases. Example: toxicity classifiers penalise African American English (AAE) dialect disproportionately.</li><li><strong>Feedback loops</strong>: a biased prediction influences future data collection (e.g., predictive policing sending more patrols to flagged areas ? more arrests in those areas ? more training data confirming the flag).</li><li><strong>Proxy variables</strong>: the model learns to use zip code as a proxy for race, even if race is not a training feature.</li></ul>' },
    { type: 'code', lang: 'python', src: '# Measuring model fairness across demographic groups\nimport numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.metrics import (confusion_matrix, accuracy_score,\n                              precision_score, recall_score)\n\ndef fairness_audit(y_true, y_pred, y_prob, sensitive_attr):\n    """\n    Compute fairness metrics across groups defined by sensitive_attr.\n    y_true, y_pred, y_prob: numpy arrays\n    sensitive_attr: array of group labels (e.g., [\'M\',\'F\',\'M\',...] )\n    """\n    from sklearn.metrics import roc_auc_score\n    groups = np.unique(sensitive_attr)\n    results = {}\n    for group in groups:\n        mask = sensitive_attr == group\n        yt, yp, yprob = y_true[mask], y_pred[mask], y_prob[mask]\n        tn, fp, fn, tp = confusion_matrix(yt, yp).ravel()\n        results[group] = {\n            "n":           mask.sum(),\n            "accuracy":    accuracy_score(yt, yp),\n            "precision":   precision_score(yt, yp),\n            "recall":      recall_score(yt, yp),    # True Positive Rate\n            "fpr":         fp / (fp + tn),           # False Positive Rate\n            "auc":         roc_auc_score(yt, yprob)\n        }\n\n    import pandas as pd\n    df = pd.DataFrame(results).T\n    print("\\nFairness Audit:")\n    print(df.round(4).to_string())\n\n    # Equalized Odds: check if TPR and FPR are equal across groups\n    tpr_gap = df["recall"].max() - df["recall"].min()\n    fpr_gap = df["fpr"].max()    - df["fpr"].min()\n    print(f"\\nMax TPR gap: {tpr_gap:.4f}  (Equalized Odds requires ~0)")\n    print(f"Max FPR gap: {fpr_gap:.4f}")\n    return df' },
    { type: 'text', body: '<h3>Fairness Metrics</h3><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.9rem"><thead><tr><th style="border:1px solid #444;padding:6px">Metric</th><th style="border:1px solid #444;padding:6px">Definition</th><th style="border:1px solid #444;padding:6px">When to use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="border:1px solid #444;padding:6px">Demographic Parity</td><td style="border:1px solid #444;padding:6px">Equal prediction rate across groups</td><td style="border:1px solid #444;padding:6px">When base rates are similar</td></tr><tr><td style="border:1px solid #444;padding:6px">Equal Opportunity</td><td style="border:1px solid #444;padding:6px">Equal True Positive Rate (recall) across groups</td><td style="border:1px solid #444;padding:6px">When false negatives are most harmful</td></tr><tr><td style="border:1px solid #444;padding:6px">Equalized Odds</td><td style="border:1px solid #444;padding:6px">Equal TPR AND FPR across groups</td><td style="border:1px solid #444;padding:6px">When both FN and FP are harmful</td></tr><tr><td style="border:1px solid #444;padding:6px">Calibration</td><td style="border:1px solid #444;padding:6px">Predicted probabilities match true rates per group</td><td style="border:1px solid #444;padding:6px">When probability scores are used for decisions</td></tr></tbody></table>' },
    { type: 'tip', title: 'Fairness and accuracy tradeoffs are real', body: 'Imposing fairness constraints almost always reduces overall accuracy � this is a fundamental mathematical result. The practitioner\'s job is to make this tradeoff explicit and intentional rather than hiding it. Document who bears the cost of errors, who benefits from the model, and which stakeholders reviewed the fairness audit before deployment.' }
  ]
};

L['dl-w7-l5'] = {
  duration_mins: 55,
  sections: [
    { type: 'text', body: '<h2>DL Research & Staying Current</h2><p>Deep learning moves faster than any other engineering field. A technique published in January can be state-of-art, widespread, and superseded all within the same year. This lesson covers how to navigate the research landscape efficiently and build practices that keep you current without spending your entire working life reading papers.</p>' },
    { type: 'text', body: '<h3>Primary Research Sources</h3><ul><li><strong>arXiv.org</strong> (cs.LG, cs.CV, cs.CL): the primary preprint server. Top papers appear here before peer review. Follow daily digest via arXiv email, Papers with Code, or Twitter/X.</li><li><strong>Conference proceedings</strong>: NeurIPS, ICML, ICLR (theory/methods); CVPR, ECCV, ICCV (vision); ACL, EMNLP (NLP). All proceedings freely available.</li><li><strong>Papers with Code</strong> (paperswithcode.com): every paper with code, organised by benchmark. Find SOTA for any task instantly.</li><li><strong>Hugging Face</strong>: model hub, datasets, spaces � the fastest way to run any published model.</li></ul>' },
    { type: 'text', body: '<h3>How to Read a DL Paper Efficiently</h3><p>Most papers can be assessed in 20 minutes using the three-pass method:</p><ol><li><strong>Pass 1 (5 min)</strong>: read title, abstract, introduction, section headings, conclusion. Answer: What problem? What\'s new? Does it matter to me?</li><li><strong>Pass 2 (10 min)</strong>: read figures, tables, and captions carefully. Understand the main result without following every derivation.</li><li><strong>Pass 3 (as needed)</strong>: deep read of methodology if you want to implement it. Code first � reading code is often faster than reading math.</li></ol>' },
    { type: 'code', lang: 'python', src: '# Tools for staying current\n\n# 1. Papers with Code API\nimport requests\n\ndef get_sota_for_task(task="image-classification", dataset="imagenet"):\n    url = f"https://paperswithcode.com/api/v1/sota/"\n    r = requests.get(url, params={"task": task, "dataset": dataset}, timeout=10)\n    results = r.json().get("results", [])[:5]\n    for i, result in enumerate(results):\n        print(f"{i+1}. {result[\'method\']}: {result[\'metrics\']}")\n\n# 2. HuggingFace Hub � find top models for any task\nfrom huggingface_hub import list_models\n\nmodels = list(list_models(task="image-classification", sort="downloads", limit=5))\nfor m in models:\n    print(f"{m.id:50s} downloads: {m.downloads:,}")\n\n# 3. Reproducibility checklist before implementing a paper\nprint("Reproducibility checklist:")\nprint("  ? Official code released? (check GitHub link in paper)")\nprint("  ? Are results reproducible with provided configs?")\nprint("  ? Same preprocessing / data split as reported?")\nprint("  ? Hyperparameters fully specified, or require search?")\nprint("  ? Are comparison baselines from same paper or reproduced?")\nprint("  ? Does the improvement hold on your dataset / hardware?")' },
    { type: 'text', body: '<h3>Which Papers to Actually Read</h3><p>Focus on: (1) papers that introduce <em>concepts</em> you will use for years (Attention, ResNets, BERT, DDPM, LoRA) � read deeply; (2) benchmark-setting papers in your domain � understand results and experimental setup; (3) papers that introduce tools you will use (PyTorch, Hugging Face, ultralytics YOLO). Skip: incremental improvements on benchmarks you do not care about; papers with no released code; workshops papers from unknown venues. The best filter: is this paper cited by papers you already trust?</p>' },
    { type: 'tip', title: 'Implement to understand', body: 'The deepest way to understand a paper is to implement it from scratch. You will catch every ambiguity, discover implementation details the paper glosses over, and develop intuition that reading alone never provides. The Annotated Transformer, makemore series by Karpathy, and fast.ai notebooks all demonstrate this approach. Budget one implementation per month for the most impactful recent papers.' }
  ]
};

